window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "matrix-calculus", "lessons": {"shapes": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-need-more-than-one-kind-of-derivative\" data-h=\"3\">Why we need more than one kind of derivative</h3>\n<p>In single-variable calculus everything is one number. You have an input <span class=\"m\">x</span>, an output <span class=\"m\">y = f(x)</span>, and the derivative <span class=\"m\">f'(x)</span> tells you how fast <span class=\"m\">y</span> changes when you nudge <span class=\"m\">x</span>. That picture is perfect for a ball rolling down a ramp, but it is far too small for machine learning.</p>\n<p>Imagine you run a small bakery and you are trying to set the prices of 40 different products. Your weekly profit depends on all 40 prices at once. If someone asks \"how sensitive is profit to the price of croissants?\", that is one ordinary derivative. But you really want the answer for every product at the same time, neatly organised so you can adjust all 40 prices in one go. The natural way to store those 40 answers is in a list with the same layout as your price list.</p>\n<p>A neural network is the same story with millions of knobs instead of 40. Its parameters live in vectors and matrices, and its loss is a single number. Matrix calculus is simply the bookkeeping that tells us how to arrange all those partial derivatives so that the formulas stay short and the shapes line up. Nothing in this course needs new calculus. Every result comes from ordinary partial derivatives, organised cleverly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-four-basic-cases\" data-h=\"3\">The four basic cases</h3>\n<p>There are four combinations that come up constantly, depending on whether the input and output are single numbers or vectors.</p>\n<p><strong>Scalar in, scalar out.</strong> This is the familiar derivative <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dy}{dx}</span>, one number.</p>\n<p><strong>Vector in, scalar out.</strong> Here <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x)</span> takes <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span> and returns one number. There are <span class=\"m\">n</span> partial derivatives, one per input, and we stack them into the <strong>gradient</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf x)</span>, a vector in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>. For example, if <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = x_1^2 + 3x_2</span> then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} = 2x_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} = 3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x_1, 3)</span>. At the point <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span> the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span>: nudging <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> by a tiny amount <span class=\"m\">h</span> raises <span class=\"m\">f</span> by about <span class=\"m\">2h</span>, and nudging <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> raises it by about <span class=\"m\">3h</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Scalar in, vector out.</strong> A curve <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y(t)</span> in space has a velocity <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d\\mathbf y}{dt}</span>, which is just the vector of derivatives of each coordinate. If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y(t) = (t^2, 5t)</span> then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d\\mathbf y}{dt} = (2t, 5)</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Vector in, vector out.</strong> Now <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = g(\\mathbf x)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb{R}^m</span>. Every output can depend on every input, so there are <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> partial derivatives. We arrange them in an <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> grid called the <strong>Jacobian</strong>, with row <span class=\"m\">i</span> holding the derivatives of output <span class=\"m\">y_i</span> and column <span class=\"m\">j</span> holding the derivatives with respect to input <span class=\"m\">x_j</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial y_i}{\\partial x_j}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (x_1x_2,\\ x_1 + x_2,\\ x_2^2)</span> we have three outputs and two inputs, so <span class=\"m\">J</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span>. Its first row is <span class=\"m\">(x_2, x_1)</span>, its second row is <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>, and its third row is <span class=\"m\">(0, 2x_2)</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">g:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^m</span>, the <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix of all first partial derivatives, <span class=\"m\">J_{ij} = \\partial y_i/\\partial x_j</span>. Row <span class=\"m\">i</span> is the gradient of output <span class=\"m\">i</span> (written as a row).</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient (of a scalar)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">f:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}</span>, the vector <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1},\\dots,\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)</span>, the same shape as <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>. It points in the direction of steepest increase.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"derivatives-with-respect-to-a-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">Derivatives with respect to a matrix</h3>\n<p>Machine learning adds one more important case: a scalar loss <span class=\"m\">L</span> that depends on a whole matrix of weights <span class=\"m\">W</span> with <span class=\"m\">m</span> rows and <span class=\"m\">n</span> columns. Each entry <span class=\"m\">W_{ij}</span> is just another variable, so there are <span class=\"m\">mn</span> partial derivatives <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}}</span>. We store them in a matrix of the same size as <span class=\"m\">W</span>, placing the derivative with respect to <span class=\"m\">W_{ij}</span> in position <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span>.</p>\n<p>A tiny example makes this concrete. Let <span class=\"m\">L = \\sum_{i,j}W_{ij}^2</span>, the sum of squares of all entries. Each partial derivative is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}} = 2W_{ij}</span>, so the whole thing is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = 2W</span>. If <span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span>, so is <span class=\"m\">2W</span>.</p>\n<p>The truly awkward case is a vector output with a matrix input. That derivative has three indices (output, row, column), a three-dimensional array. In practice we never build it. Backpropagation is designed so that every derivative we actually store is a scalar's derivative, which keeps everything two-dimensional at most.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-shape-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The shape rule</h3>\n<p>Here is the single most useful habit in this whole course.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key idea</div><div class=\"box-title\">The shape rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The gradient of a scalar with respect to anything has <strong>the same shape as that thing</strong>. <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}</span> is the same shape as <span class=\"m\">W</span>, so the update <span class=\"m\">W \\leftarrow W - \\eta\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}</span> makes sense. When you derive a formula, check that its shape matches. A wrong shape always means a wrong answer.</p></div></div>\n<p>The rule is not just tidy, it is how optimizers work. Gradient descent subtracts a small multiple of the gradient from the parameter, entry by entry. That only works if the two arrays line up exactly.</p>\n<p>The shape rule also lets you guess answers. Suppose <span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span>, and <span class=\"m\">L = \\|W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span>. The answer must be <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>. The natural ingredients are the residual <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y</span> (size <span class=\"m\">m</span>) and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> (size <span class=\"m\">n</span>). The only way to combine an <span class=\"m\">m</span>-vector and an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-vector into an <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix is the outer product <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>. The true answer is indeed <span class=\"m\">2\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>, as you will derive later. The other ordering, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T</span>, is <span class=\"m\">n\\times m</span> and must be wrong.</p>\n<h3 id=\"layout-conventions\" data-h=\"3\">Layout conventions</h3>\n<p>Different books store these derivatives differently, and this causes endless confusion. In <strong>numerator layout</strong> the Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> (outputs index the rows), as we defined above. In <strong>denominator layout</strong> it is the transpose, <span class=\"m\">n\\times m</span>. Neither is wrong, but you must not mix them in one derivation.</p>\n<p>In this course we use numerator layout for Jacobians and \"same shape as the variable\" for gradients of scalars. That pairing is what PyTorch, JAX and NumPy code effectively uses. One consequence is worth remembering: for a scalar function, the Jacobian is a <span class=\"m\">1\\times n</span> row, and the gradient is its transpose, an <span class=\"m\">n\\times1</span> column.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most common mistake is getting the transpose wrong, for example writing <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>. A shape check catches it instantly. The second mistake is copying a formula from a book that uses the other layout convention, so that every Jacobian comes out transposed. The third is forgetting that a gradient describes the change for a <strong>tiny</strong> nudge: if <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{23}} = 0.5</span>, raising <span class=\"m\">W_{23}</span> by <span class=\"m\">0.02</span> changes <span class=\"m\">L</span> by about <span class=\"m\">0.01</span>, but raising it by <span class=\"m\">10</span> need not change <span class=\"m\">L</span> by <span class=\"m\">5</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A derivative collects every \"how much does this output move when I nudge that input\" number. A scalar's gradient has the input's shape. A vector function's Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>, outputs by inputs. A scalar's derivative with respect to a matrix is a matrix of the same size. Checking shapes is the fastest way to catch errors in any derivation you will meet.</p>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Output \\ Input</th>\n<th>scalar <span class=\"m\">x</span></th>\n<th>vector <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span></th>\n<th>matrix <span class=\"m\">W\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}</span></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>scalar <span class=\"m\">y</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{dy}{dx}</span> scalar</td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}y\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial W}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}</span></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vector <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb{R}^m</span></td>\n<td><span class=\"m\">\\frac{d\\mathbf y}{dx}\\in\\mathbb{R}^m</span></td>\n<td>Jacobian <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span></td>\n<td>3-D array (avoid)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the point. The pink arrow is the gradient: one number per input coordinate.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the point to <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, then to <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>. Read off the gradient each time.\n2. Drag it to the origin and watch the gradient shrink to <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>.\n3. Switch the surface to the saddle and repeat.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the function <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 2y^2</span> takes two inputs and returns one number, so its gradient has exactly two entries, the same shape as the input <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span>. That is the shape rule in action. At <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2, 0)</span> and at <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> it is <span class=\"m\">(0, 4)</span>, matching <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x, 4y)</span>. Because the gradient lives in the same space as the input, you can draw it as an arrow on the input plane and step against it, which is exactly what gradient descent does.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf x) = \\left(\\tfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1},\\dots,\\tfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)^T\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial y_i}{\\partial x_j},\\qquad J\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}\\ \\text{for}\\ g:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\left(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}\\right)_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}},\\qquad \\Delta L\\approx\\sum_{i,j}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}}\\Delta W_{ij}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">First-order change for matrix variables</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is differentiable in <span class=\"m\">W</span>, then for a small change <span class=\"m\">\\Delta W</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">L(W + \\Delta W)\\approx L(W) + \\sum_{i,j}\\left(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}\\right)_{ij}\\Delta W_{ij} = L(W) + \\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(\\left(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}\\right)^T\\Delta W\\right).</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Why</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Treat the <span class=\"m\">mn</span> entries of <span class=\"m\">W</span> as one long vector of variables. The ordinary multivariable linear approximation says the change in <span class=\"m\">L</span> is the sum, over every variable, of its partial derivative times its change. Arranging those partials in the shape of <span class=\"m\">W</span> and using <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(A^TB) = \\sum_{ij}A_{ij}B_{ij}</span> gives the trace form.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the gradient of <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = x_1^2x_2 + 3x_2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Differentiate with respect to <span class=\"m\">x_1</span>, treating <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> as a constant: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} = 2x_1x_2</span>.</li>\n<li>Differentiate with respect to <span class=\"m\">x_2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} = x_1^2 + 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Stack them: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x_1x_2,\\ x_1^2 + 3)</span>.</li>\n<li>Plug in <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (4, 4)</span>. Two inputs, two entries.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find the Jacobian of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (x_1x_2,\\ x_1 + x_2,\\ x_2^2)</span> at <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Three outputs, two inputs, so <span class=\"m\">J</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span>.</li>\n<li>Row 1: <span class=\"m\">\\left(\\frac{\\partial(x_1x_2)}{\\partial x_1}, \\frac{\\partial(x_1x_2)}{\\partial x_2}\\right) = (x_2, x_1) = (3, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Row 2: <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. Row 3: <span class=\"m\">(0, 2x_2) = (0, 6)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 2\\\\1 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use shapes to pick the right formula for <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W</span>, with <span class=\"m\">L = \\|W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">W</span> of size <span class=\"m\">4\\times3</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The answer must be <span class=\"m\">4\\times3</span>.</li>\n<li>The residual <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y</span> has size 4 and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> has size 3.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span> is <span class=\"m\">(4\\times1)(1\\times3) = 4\\times3</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times4</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r^T\\mathbf r</span> is <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span>.</li>\n<li>Only <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span> (times a constant) can be right. The exact answer is <span class=\"m\">2\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(W) = \\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = (1, -1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v = (2, 0, 3)</span>. (a) What shape is <span class=\"m\">W</span>, and what shape is <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial W</span>? (b) Compute <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial W</span>. (c) If <span class=\"m\">W_{13}</span> increases by <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>, by how much does <span class=\"m\">f</span> change?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v</span> must be <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span>, and so is <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial W</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">f = \\sum_{i,j}u_iW_{ij}v_j</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial W_{ij}} = u_iv_j</span>. That is the outer product <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0 &amp; 3\\\\-2 &amp; 0 &amp; -3\\end{bmatrix}</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial W_{13}} = 3</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f</span> changes by <span class=\"m\">3\\times0.1 = 0.3</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">f</span> is linear in <span class=\"m\">W</span> this is exact, not just approximate.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every parameter tensor in PyTorch has a <code>.grad</code> attribute with exactly the same shape as the parameter. A linear layer with weight of shape <code>(out, in)</code> gets a gradient of shape <code>(out, in)</code>, and the optimizer step <code>p -= lr * p.grad</code> is the shape rule in code. When you write a custom layer, asserting <code>grad.shape == param.shape</code> catches most bugs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Shape errors are the most common runtime error in numerical code. Libraries like NumPy check shapes when you multiply arrays, and tools such as <code>jaxtyping</code> let you annotate functions with shapes like <code>Float[Array, \"batch in\"]</code> so that mismatches are caught early.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A shop that sets prices for many products can estimate how weekly profit changes when each price moves by one cent. Those sensitivities form a list with the same layout as the price list, which is a gradient. A manager can then raise the prices with positive sensitivity and lower those with negative sensitivity, one small step at a time.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">L</span> is a scalar and <span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">64\\times 128</span>. What is the shape of <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">64\\times128</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">128\\times64</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">8192\\times1</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>By the shape rule, the gradient of a scalar has the same shape as the variable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb{R}^3</span> depends on <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^5</span>. In numerator layout, what is the shape of the Jacobian <span class=\"m\">\\partial\\mathbf y/\\partial\\mathbf x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">5\\times3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">15\\times1</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Rows are outputs and columns are inputs, so there are 3 rows and 5 columns.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = x_1^2 + 3x_2</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial x_1</span> at the point <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial x_1 = 2x_1 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many entries does the Jacobian of a function from <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^4</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^{10}</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"40\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is <span class=\"m\">10\\times4</span>, one entry for every (output, input) pair.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">W</span> is <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta\\in\\mathbb{R}^m</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span>. Which expression has the right shape to be <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta\\mathbf x^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\boldsymbol\\delta^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta^T\\mathbf x</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(m\\times1)(1\\times n) = m\\times n</span>, the shape of <span class=\"m\">W</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_{23} = 0.5</span>. If you increase <span class=\"m\">W_{23}</span> by <span class=\"m\">0.02</span> and leave the rest alone, by about how much does <span class=\"m\">L</span> change?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.01\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>First-order change: <span class=\"m\">0.5\\times0.02 = 0.01</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "mc-jacobians": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"zooming-in-until-things-look-straight\" data-h=\"3\">Zooming in until things look straight</h3>\n<p>Take any smooth curve, say the graph of <span class=\"m\">y = x^2</span>, and zoom in on one point. The more you zoom, the straighter the curve looks, until it is indistinguishable from its tangent line. That is the deepest idea in calculus: smooth functions are <strong>locally linear</strong>. In one variable we write it as\n<span class=\"mb\">f(x + h)\\approx f(x) + f'(x)\\,h</span>\nThe derivative <span class=\"m\">f'(x)</span> is the slope of the straight line that the function resembles near <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p>\n<p>Matrix calculus asks the same question for functions that take a vector in and give a vector out. What is the \"straight\" thing such a function resembles when you zoom in? It cannot be a single slope, because the output can move in different directions depending on which way you nudge the input. The answer is a <strong>matrix</strong>, and that matrix is the Jacobian.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-picture-a-rubber-sheet\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday picture: a rubber sheet</h3>\n<p>Imagine printing a square grid on a sheet of rubber and then stretching and twisting the sheet. Far away, the grid lines become curved and the squares become odd shapes. But look at one tiny square under a magnifying glass. It has become a tiny <strong>parallelogram</strong>, with nearly straight sides. A parallelogram is exactly what a matrix does to a square. So near each point, the stretched sheet behaves like a linear transformation, and the matrix of that transformation is the Jacobian at that point.</p>\n<p>Written as a formula, for <span class=\"m\">F:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^m</span> near a point <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">F(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx F(\\mathbf p) + J(\\mathbf p)\\,\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">J(\\mathbf p)</span> is the <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> Jacobian evaluated at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span>. This is the one-variable formula with <span class=\"m\">f'(x)</span> replaced by a matrix and <span class=\"m\">h</span> replaced by a small vector.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linearization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The approximation <span class=\"m\">F(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx F(\\mathbf p) + J(\\mathbf p)\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>, valid for small <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>. It replaces a curved map by the best straight (affine) map near <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-worked-example-with-numbers\" data-h=\"3\">A worked example with numbers</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = (x^2 - y^2,\\ 2xy)</span>. The four partial derivatives are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2 - y^2) = 2x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2 - y^2) = -2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(2xy) = 2y</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2xy) = 2x</span>. So\n<span class=\"mb\">J(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x &amp; -2y\\\\ 2y &amp; 2x\\end{bmatrix}</span>\nAt <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p = (1, 0.5)</span> we get <span class=\"m\">F(\\mathbf p) = (1 - 0.25,\\ 1) = (0.75, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<p>Now nudge the input by <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.1, 0)</span>. The linear prediction is <span class=\"m\">F(\\mathbf p) + J\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.75, 1) + (0.2, 0.1) = (0.95, 1.1)</span>. The true value is <span class=\"m\">F(1.1, 0.5) = (1.21 - 0.25,\\ 1.1) = (0.96, 1.1)</span>. The prediction is off by only <span class=\"m\">0.01</span>, and that error shrinks like the <strong>square</strong> of the step size: halve the step and the error falls to about a quarter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-columns-mean\" data-h=\"3\">What the columns mean</h3>\n<p>Look at <span class=\"m\">J\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span> when <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span> points along one axis. If <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (h, 0)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">J\\boldsymbol\\Delta = h\\times(\\text{first column of }J)</span>. So the first column tells you where the output moves when you nudge the first input, and the second column does the same for the second input. In the example the first column <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> says \"moving <span class=\"m\">x</span> to the right pushes the output right by twice as much and up by the same amount\".</p>\n<p>This is the same story as in linear algebra, where a matrix's columns are the images of the basis vectors. The Jacobian is the matrix of the local linear map, so its columns are the images of tiny steps along each axis.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-the-determinant-means\" data-h=\"3\">What the determinant means</h3>\n<p>For a square Jacobian (<span class=\"m\">m = n</span>), the determinant measures how the map scales area (or volume) locally. A tiny square of area <span class=\"m\">a</span> near <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> is mapped to a tiny parallelogram of area about <span class=\"m\">|\\det J(\\mathbf p)|\\,a</span>. For our example <span class=\"m\">\\det J = 4x^2 + 4y^2</span>, which at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span> is <span class=\"m\">4 + 1 = 5</span>. Little squares near that point come out five times bigger.</p>\n<p>A classic case is polar coordinates, <span class=\"m\">F(r, \\theta) = (r\\cos\\theta,\\ r\\sin\\theta)</span>. Its Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -r\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; r\\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}</span>, whose determinant is <span class=\"m\">r\\cos^2\\theta + r\\sin^2\\theta = r</span>. That is why the area element in polar coordinates is <span class=\"m\">r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>: rectangles in the <span class=\"m\">(r, \\theta)</span> plane get stretched by a factor <span class=\"m\">r</span>.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\det J(\\mathbf p) = 0</span>, the map squashes the neighbourhood flat, and it cannot be undone locally. If <span class=\"m\">\\det J &lt; 0</span> the map flips orientation, like a mirror.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linear-maps-have-a-constant-jacobian\" data-h=\"3\">Linear maps have a constant Jacobian</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x</span> for a fixed matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, then <span class=\"m\">F_i = \\sum_jA_{ij}x_j</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial x_j} = A_{ij}</span>. So <span class=\"m\">J = A</span> everywhere. A linear map is its own linearization, which is a good sanity check for any formula you derive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-hessian-the-jacobian-of-the-gradient\" data-h=\"3\">The Hessian: the Jacobian of the gradient</h3>\n<p>For a scalar function <span class=\"m\">f:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}</span>, the gradient <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> is itself a function from <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb{R}^n</span>. Its Jacobian is an <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix of second derivatives, called the <strong>Hessian</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\,\\partial x_j}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">f = x^2y + y^3</span> the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2xy,\\ x^2 + 3y^2)</span>, and differentiating again gives <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2y &amp; 2x\\\\2x &amp; 6y\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The off-diagonal entries are equal. For smooth functions this is always true, so the Hessian is symmetric. The Hessian describes curvature, and it reappears in Newton's method and in the test for whether a critical point is a minimum.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hessian matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">f:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}</span>, the <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> symmetric matrix of second partial derivatives <span class=\"m\">H_{ij} = \\partial^2f/\\partial x_i\\partial x_j</span>. It is the Jacobian of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Putting inputs on the rows instead of outputs gives the transpose of the Jacobian. Forgetting that <span class=\"m\">J</span> depends on the point: a Jacobian is a matrix-valued <strong>function</strong>, so always say where you evaluate it. Reading <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> as a length scale rather than an area (or volume) scale.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Near any point, a smooth map <span class=\"m\">F</span> behaves like the linear map <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta\\mapsto J\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>. The columns of <span class=\"m\">J</span> are the images of tiny steps along each input axis, <span class=\"m\">|\\det J|</span> is the local area scale, a linear map's Jacobian is the matrix itself, and the Hessian is the Jacobian of the gradient. Backpropagation is built entirely from these local linear maps.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-jacobian\" data-args='{&quot;map&quot;: &quot;square&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the pink point in the input plane, then shrink the square. Blue is the true image; orange dashed is the Jacobian's prediction.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Leave the point at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span> and read <span class=\"m\">J</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> from the panel. Compare with the article.\n2. Drag the square-size slider from 1.5 down to 0.1 and watch the blue and orange grids merge.\n3. Move the point close to the origin and watch <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> approach 0.\n4. Switch the map to \"polar\" and check that <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> equals the first coordinate <span class=\"m\">r</span>.\n5. Switch to \"linear\" and drag the point anywhere.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> big squares come out curved, but small squares come out as parallelograms that match the orange prediction. The \"largest gap\" number drops roughly fourfold every time you halve the square, which is the second-order error in the theorem below. For the linear map the gap is always zero and <span class=\"m\">J</span> never changes, because a linear map is its own linearization.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">F(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx F(\\mathbf p) + J(\\mathbf p)\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\\ \\Rightarrow\\ J = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j},\\qquad H = J_{\\nabla f}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbf x + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx f(\\mathbf x) + \\nabla f^T\\boldsymbol\\Delta + \\tfrac12\\boldsymbol\\Delta^TH\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{area}(F(S))\\approx|\\det J|\\cdot\\text{area}(S)\\ \\text{for a small region } S</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The linearization error is second order</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">F</span> has continuous second derivatives near <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span>, there is a constant <span class=\"m\">C</span> such that <span class=\"m\">\\|F(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta) - F(\\mathbf p) - J(\\mathbf p)\\boldsymbol\\Delta\\|\\le C\\|\\boldsymbol\\Delta\\|^2</span> for all small <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Apply the one-variable Taylor theorem with remainder to each component along the segment from <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>: <span class=\"m\">F_i(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta) = F_i(\\mathbf p) + \\nabla F_i(\\mathbf p)^T\\boldsymbol\\Delta + \\frac12\\boldsymbol\\Delta^TH_i(\\boldsymbol\\xi)\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span> for some point <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\xi</span> on the segment. The rows <span class=\"m\">\\nabla F_i^T</span> form <span class=\"m\">J</span>, and the remainders are bounded by <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\max\\|H_i\\|\\,\\|\\boldsymbol\\Delta\\|^2</span>. Combining the components gives the bound.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Symmetry of second derivatives (Schwarz)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> has continuous second partial derivatives, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x_j\\partial x_i}</span>, so the Hessian is symmetric.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian and area scale of polar coordinates at <span class=\"m\">(r, \\theta) = (2, \\pi/2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -r\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; r\\cos\\theta\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">\\theta = \\pi/2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sin\\theta = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; -2\\\\1 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Interpret the columns: nudging <span class=\"m\">r</span> moves the point straight up (radially outward at the top of the circle); nudging <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> moves it left by twice as much (along the circle of radius 2).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\det J = 0\\cdot0 - (-2)(1) = 2 = r</span>. Small regions are stretched by a factor of 2 in area.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Approximate <span class=\"m\">F(1.1, 0.5)</span> for <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = (x^2 - y^2, 2xy)</span> using the Jacobian at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">F(1, 0.5) = (0.75, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; -1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.1, 0)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">J\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.2, 0.1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Prediction: <span class=\"m\">(0.95, 1.1)</span>. True value: <span class=\"m\">(0.96, 1.1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Error <span class=\"m\">0.01 = (0.1)^2</span>, consistent with second-order error.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hessian of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2y + y^3</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Gradient: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2xy,\\ x^2 + 3y^2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Differentiate each component again: <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2y &amp; 2x\\\\2x &amp; 6y\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}</span>. It is symmetric, as Schwarz's theorem promises.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = (xy,\\ x + y^2)</span>. (a) Find the Jacobian at <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>. (b) Use it to approximate <span class=\"m\">F(2.1, 0.9)</span> and compare with the exact value. (c) What does <span class=\"m\">\\det J(2, 1)</span> tell you?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{bmatrix}y &amp; x\\\\1 &amp; 2y\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">J(2, 1) = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">F(2, 1) = (2, 3)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.1, -0.1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">J\\boldsymbol\\Delta = (0.1 - 0.2,\\ 0.1 - 0.2) = (-0.1, -0.1)</span>. Prediction <span class=\"m\">(1.9, 2.9)</span>. Exact: <span class=\"m\">(2.1\\times0.9,\\ 2.1 + 0.81) = (1.89, 2.91)</span>. Both coordinates are off by <span class=\"m\">0.01</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">\\det J = 1\\cdot2 - 2\\cdot1 = 0</span>. Near <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> the map squashes small regions onto (almost) a line, so it is not locally invertible there.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every layer of a network is a vector function, and its Jacobian says how a small change in its input changes its output. <strong>Normalizing flows</strong> generate data by passing noise through invertible layers and use the change-of-variables rule <span class=\"m\">\\log p(\\mathbf x) = \\log p(\\mathbf z) - \\log|\\det J|</span>, so they are designed to have cheap Jacobian determinants. The Hessian of the loss describes the curvature of the training landscape and governs which learning rates are stable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Robot arms use the Jacobian of the map from joint angles to hand position. To move the hand by a small <span class=\"m\">\\Delta\\mathbf p</span>, the controller solves <span class=\"m\">J\\Delta\\boldsymbol\\theta = \\Delta\\mathbf p</span> for the joint changes. Points where <span class=\"m\">\\det J = 0</span> are called <strong>singularities</strong>: the arm cannot move its hand in some direction there.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Flat world maps distort area. The Mercator projection's Jacobian has determinant that grows like <span class=\"m\">1/\\cos^2(\\text{latitude})</span>, which is why Greenland looks as large as Africa even though Africa is about 14 times bigger.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x</span> where <span class=\"m\">A</span> is a fixed <span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span> matrix. What is the Jacobian of <span class=\"m\">F</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">A</span>, at every point</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It depends on <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">F_i = \\sum_jA_{ij}x_j</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\partial F_i/\\partial x_j = A_{ij}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = (x^2 - y^2, 2xy)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">J(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\det J = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In polar coordinates <span class=\"m\">F(r, \\theta) = (r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> at <span class=\"m\">r = 3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det J = r</span> for every <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 3xy</span>, what is the Hessian entry <span class=\"m\">H_{12} = \\partial^2f/\\partial x\\partial y</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial x = 2x + 3y</span>, and differentiating with respect to <span class=\"m\">y</span> gives 3.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = xy</span>, use the linearization at <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span> to estimate <span class=\"m\">f(2.1, 3)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6.3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (y, x) = (3, 2)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f\\approx6 + 3(0.1) + 2(0) = 6.3</span>. Here it is exact, since <span class=\"m\">f</span> is linear in <span class=\"m\">x</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\det J(\\mathbf p) = 0</span>, then near <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> the map...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">squashes small regions to zero area, so it is not locally invertible</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">preserves area exactly</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">is a rotation</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A zero determinant means the local linear map collapses at least one direction.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "identities": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-learn-a-few-identities-by-heart\" data-h=\"3\">Why learn a few identities by heart</h3>\n<p>When you learned arithmetic, you memorised the times tables so you would not have to count on your fingers every time. Matrix calculus has its own small times table: about ten gradient formulas that appear in almost every derivation in machine learning. Knowing them lets you differentiate a loss like <span class=\"m\">\\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2 + \\lambda\\|\\mathbf w\\|^2</span> in one line instead of a page.</p>\n<p>But you should never treat them as magic. Every one of them can be derived in a few lines with one reliable method, and we will derive them all here. If you forget one, you can rebuild it in a minute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-universal-method-go-to-indices\" data-h=\"3\">The universal method: go to indices</h3>\n<p>Any expression built from vectors and matrices can be written as a sum over indices. Once it is a sum of ordinary numbers, you differentiate with respect to one coordinate using normal calculus, and then pack the answers back into a vector or matrix. The method has three steps: <strong>expand</strong> into a sum, <strong>differentiate</strong> with respect to <span class=\"m\">x_k</span> (or <span class=\"m\">W_{kl}</span>), <strong>re-pack</strong> into matrix form.</p>\n<p>The one fact you need is how to differentiate a variable with respect to another: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial x_i}{\\partial x_k}</span> is 1 when <span class=\"m\">i = k</span> and 0 otherwise. So in a sum, only the terms that contain <span class=\"m\">x_k</span> survive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"linear-functions-mathph13x\" data-h=\"3\">Linear functions: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x</span></h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x = \\sum_ia_ix_i</span>. Differentiating with respect to <span class=\"m\">x_k</span>, every term except <span class=\"m\">a_kx_k</span> disappears, leaving <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_k} = a_k</span>. Packing these up, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a</span>. With <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a = (1, -2, 5)</span>, <span class=\"m\">f = x_1 - 2x_2 + 5x_3</span> and the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(1, -2, 5)</span>, the same at every point. This is the vector version of \"the derivative of <span class=\"m\">ax</span> is <span class=\"m\">a</span>\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"squared-length-mathph24x\" data-h=\"3\">Squared length: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x</span></h3>\n<p>Now <span class=\"m\">f = \\sum_ix_i^2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_k} = 2x_k</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\nabla(\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x) = 2\\mathbf x</span>, the vector version of \"the derivative of <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> is <span class=\"m\">2x</span>\". At <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (3, 4)</span> the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(6, 8)</span>, pointing straight away from the origin, which makes sense: squared length grows fastest when you move outward. The plain length <span class=\"m\">\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\sqrt{\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x}</span> has gradient <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\mathbf x}{\\|\\mathbf x\\|}</span> by the chain rule, a unit vector pointing outward.</p>\n<h3 id=\"quadratic-forms-mathph34x\" data-h=\"3\">Quadratic forms: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x</span></h3>\n<p>This one matters most, because every quadratic loss has this form. Write <span class=\"m\">f = \\sum_i\\sum_jx_iA_{ij}x_j</span>. The variable <span class=\"m\">x_k</span> appears in two ways: as the left factor (when <span class=\"m\">i = k</span>), giving <span class=\"m\">\\sum_jA_{kj}x_j = (A\\mathbf x)_k</span>, and as the right factor (when <span class=\"m\">j = k</span>), giving <span class=\"m\">\\sum_ix_iA_{ik} = (A^T\\mathbf x)_k</span>. Adding the two,\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x) = (A + A^T)\\mathbf x</span>\nWhen <span class=\"m\">A</span> is symmetric this is <span class=\"m\">2A\\mathbf x</span>, the vector version of \"the derivative of <span class=\"m\">ax^2</span> is <span class=\"m\">2ax</span>\".</p>\n<p>Check it with numbers. Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Expanding, <span class=\"m\">f = x_1^2 + 2x_1x_2 + 3x_2^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} = 2x_1 + 2x_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} = 2x_1 + 6x_2</span>. At <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> that is <span class=\"m\">(4, 8)</span>. The formula gives <span class=\"m\">(A + A^T)\\mathbf x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix} = (4, 8)</span>. They agree. Notice that <span class=\"m\">2A\\mathbf x = (6, 6)</span> would be wrong here, because this <span class=\"m\">A</span> is not symmetric.</p>\n<h3 id=\"least-squares-mathph54x\" data-h=\"3\">Least squares: <span class=\"m\">\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2</span></h3>\n<p>Expand the squared norm: <span class=\"m\">(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)^T(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b) = \\mathbf x^TA^TA\\mathbf x - 2\\mathbf b^TA\\mathbf x + \\mathbf b^T\\mathbf b</span>. Use the quadratic-form rule with the symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A^TA</span> and the linear rule with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a = A^T\\mathbf b</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2 = 2A^TA\\mathbf x - 2A^T\\mathbf b = 2A^T(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)</span>\nRead it as \"transpose of the matrix, times the residual\". This shape, <span class=\"m\">A^T\\times(\\text{error})</span>, will appear again and again in backpropagation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gradients-with-respect-to-a-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">Gradients with respect to a matrix</h3>\n<p>For <span class=\"m\">f(W) = \\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v = \\sum_{i,j}u_iW_{ij}v_j</span>, differentiating with respect to <span class=\"m\">W_{kl}</span> leaves only <span class=\"m\">u_kv_l</span>. Packing into a matrix gives the outer product <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T</span>. Similarly <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(AX) = \\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}X_{ji}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X_{kl}} = A_{lk}</span> and the gradient is <span class=\"m\">A^T</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"log-sum-exp-gives-softmax\" data-h=\"3\">Log-sum-exp gives softmax</h3>\n<p>In classification we often meet <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{LSE}(\\mathbf z) = \\log\\sum_je^{z_j}</span>. Differentiating with respect to <span class=\"m\">z_k</span> by the chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac{e^{z_k}}{\\sum_je^{z_j}}</span>, which is exactly the <span class=\"m\">k</span>-th softmax probability. So <span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\operatorname{LSE} = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = (0, \\ln3)</span>, the exponentials are <span class=\"m\">1</span> and <span class=\"m\">3</span>, so the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(\\frac14, \\frac34)</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box tip\"><div class=\"box-label\">Tip</div><div class=\"box-title\">When stuck, go to indices</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Any matrix derivative can be done by writing everything as sums over indices, taking ordinary partial derivatives, and re-packing. It is slower than remembering an identity, but it never fails, and it is the best way to check a formula you found online.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Using <span class=\"m\">2A\\mathbf x</span> for a non-symmetric <span class=\"m\">A</span> (the correct answer is <span class=\"m\">(A + A^T)\\mathbf x</span>). Writing <span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2 = 2A(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)</span> without the transpose, which usually does not even have the right shape. Forgetting the factor 2 that comes from a square.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-a-transpose-keeps-appearing\" data-h=\"3\">Why a transpose keeps appearing</h3>\n<p>You may have noticed that transposes pop up in the answers even when the original expression had none, as in <span class=\"m\">2A^T(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)</span>. There is a simple reason. The matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> sends inputs (size <span class=\"m\">n</span>) to outputs (size <span class=\"m\">m</span>). The residual <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b</span> lives in the output space, but the gradient must live in the input space, because it has the shape of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>. The only natural way to carry a vector from the output space back to the input space is to multiply by <span class=\"m\">A^T</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">n\\times m</span>. So whenever an error measured at the output of a matrix is turned into a gradient for that matrix's input, expect a transpose. This single observation is the heart of backpropagation: errors travel backwards through each layer by multiplication with the transposed weight matrix.</p>\n<h3 id=\"combining-identities\" data-h=\"3\">Combining identities</h3>\n<p>Real losses are sums of these building blocks, and gradients add. Take <span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf w) = \\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2 + \\lambda\\|\\mathbf w\\|^2</span>, the ridge regression loss. The first term contributes <span class=\"m\">2X^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y)</span> by the least-squares identity, and the second contributes <span class=\"m\">2\\lambda\\mathbf w</span> by the squared-length identity, scaled by the constant <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>. The total gradient is simply the sum. No new calculus was needed, only two lines of the table. The same approach handles a constant factor (it multiplies the gradient), a constant offset (its gradient is zero), and sums over data points (the gradient is the sum of the individual gradients). In practice, almost every derivation in this course is \"split the loss into recognisable pieces, look up each piece, add\".</p>\n<p>It is also worth checking any combined result with a tiny example. If <span class=\"m\">X</span> is the <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">[2]</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">w = 1</span>, then <span class=\"m\">L = (2 - 4)^2 + 1 = 5</span>, and the formula gives <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot2\\cdot(2 - 4) + 2\\cdot1 = -6</span>. Differentiating <span class=\"m\">L(w) = (2w - 4)^2 + w^2</span> directly gives <span class=\"m\">4(2w - 4) + 2w = -6</span> at <span class=\"m\">w = 1</span>. A one-dimensional check like this takes seconds and catches most sign and transpose errors.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Linear terms have constant gradients (<span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span>), squares give a factor of two (<span class=\"m\">2\\mathbf x</span>), quadratic forms give <span class=\"m\">(A + A^T)\\mathbf x</span>, least squares gives <span class=\"m\">2A^T(\\text{residual})</span>, bilinear forms in a matrix give outer products, and log-sum-exp gives softmax. All of them come from the same three steps: expand, differentiate, re-pack.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The bowl is <span class=\"m\">f = \\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x</span> with <span class=\"m\">A = \\operatorname{diag}(1, 2)</span>. Drag the point and compare the arrow with <span class=\"m\">2A\\mathbf x</span>.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the point to <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>. The identity predicts <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = 2A\\mathbf x = (2, 4)</span>. Check the panel.\n2. Drag it to <span class=\"m\">(-1.5, 0.5)</span> and predict the gradient before you look: <span class=\"m\">(-3, 2)</span>.\n3. Switch to the saddle, which is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x</span> with <span class=\"m\">A = \\operatorname{diag}(1, -1)</span>, and predict <span class=\"m\">(2x, -2y)</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the gradient of a quadratic form is a <strong>linear</strong> function of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>: double the distance from the origin and the arrow doubles. On the bowl the arrow always points uphill and away from the minimum at the origin. On the saddle the second component flips sign, because the negative entry of <span class=\"m\">A</span> makes <span class=\"m\">f</span> decrease in the <span class=\"m\">y</span> direction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x) = 2\\mathbf x \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\frac{\\mathbf x}{\\|\\mathbf x\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x) = (A + A^T)\\mathbf x \\ \\ (= 2A\\mathbf x \\text{ if } A \\text{ symmetric})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial (A\\mathbf x)}{\\partial \\mathbf x} = A \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2 = 2A^T(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial W}(\\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v) = \\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T \\qquad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(AX) = A^T \\qquad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(X^TA) = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\log\\textstyle\\sum_j e^{z_j} = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Normal equations</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w</span> minimises <span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf w) = \\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\mathbf w = X^T\\mathbf y</span>. If <span class=\"m\">X</span> has independent columns, the unique minimiser is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w = (X^TX)^{-1}X^T\\mathbf y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">L</span> is a convex quadratic (its Hessian <span class=\"m\">2X^TX</span> is positive semidefinite), so a point is a minimiser exactly when the gradient is zero. Setting <span class=\"m\">\\nabla L = 2X^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y) = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\mathbf w = X^T\\mathbf y</span>. Independent columns make <span class=\"m\">X^TX</span> invertible, giving the unique solution.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derive <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x)</span> by index notation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Expand: <span class=\"m\">f = \\sum_i\\sum_j x_iA_{ij}x_j</span>.</li>\n<li>Differentiate with respect to <span class=\"m\">x_k</span>. Terms with <span class=\"m\">i = k</span> give <span class=\"m\">\\sum_j A_{kj}x_j</span>; terms with <span class=\"m\">j = k</span> give <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i x_iA_{ik}</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_k} = (A\\mathbf x)_k + (A^T\\mathbf x)_k</span>.</li>\n<li>Re-pack: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (A + A^T)\\mathbf x</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Ridge regression in three lines</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf w) = \\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2 + \\lambda\\|\\mathbf w\\|^2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla L = 2X^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y) + 2\\lambda\\mathbf w</span>.</li>\n<li>Set to zero: <span class=\"m\">(X^TX + \\lambda I)\\mathbf w = X^T\\mathbf y</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span> the matrix is always invertible.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient of log-sum-exp at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = (1, 1, 1 + \\ln 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\operatorname{LSE} = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)</span>, and softmax ignores a common shift, so subtract 1: <span class=\"m\">(0, 0, \\ln2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Exponentials: <span class=\"m\">(1, 1, 2)</span>, which sum to 4.</li>\n<li>Gradient <span class=\"m\">= (\\frac14, \\frac14, \\frac12)</span>. The entries are positive and sum to 1.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x - 2\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf x</span> with <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf b = (2, 4)</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span>, the minimiser, and the minimum value.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p><span class=\"m\">A</span> is symmetric, so <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = 2A\\mathbf x - 2\\mathbf b</span>. Setting it to zero gives <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (2/2, 4/4) = (1, 1)</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">A</span> is positive definite this is the minimum. The value is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x - 2\\mathbf b^T\\mathbf x = (2 + 4) - 2(2 + 4) = -6</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linear regression, ridge regression, Gaussian log-likelihoods, Gaussian process marginal likelihoods and the second-order Taylor model of any loss are all quadratic forms, so these identities give their gradients directly. The softmax identity is the reason the cross-entropy gradient is so simple, as you will see in the losses lesson.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A delivery company that fits travel time as a linear function of distance, traffic and weather solves exactly the normal equations <span class=\"m\">X^TX\\mathbf w = X^T\\mathbf y</span>, derived here with one identity. Spreadsheet \"trendline\" features do the same thing behind the scenes.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(3\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">6\\mathbf x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3\\mathbf x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">6</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\nabla(\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x) = 2\\mathbf x</span>, times 3.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a = (1, -2, 5)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial x_3</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The gradient of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For a non-symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x)</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2A\\mathbf x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(A + A^T)\\mathbf x</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^T\\mathbf x</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> appears on both sides of <span class=\"m\">A</span>, which produces one <span class=\"m\">A</span> term and one <span class=\"m\">A^T</span> term.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf b = (1, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 1)</span>. What is the second component of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Residual <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>; <span class=\"m\">2A^T(0, 1) = (0, 4)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial W}(\\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v)</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf v\\mathbf u^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u^T\\mathbf v</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\partial/\\partial W_{kl} = u_kv_l</span>, the <span class=\"m\">(k, l)</span> entry of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first component of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\log(e^{z_1} + e^{z_2})</span> at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = (0, 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.5|1/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{softmax}(0, 0) = (\\frac12, \\frac12)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "differentials": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"when-index-notation-gets-painful\" data-h=\"3\">When index notation gets painful</h3>\n<p>Index notation always works, but some expressions make it miserable. Try differentiating <span class=\"m\">\\log\\det\\Sigma</span> with respect to a covariance matrix <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, or <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}A)</span> with respect to <span class=\"m\">X</span>, by writing out every sum. You will fill a page with indices and probably make a mistake. Statisticians and machine learning researchers use a cleaner tool called <strong>matrix differentials</strong>. It works like the product rule you already know, plus one trick with the trace, and it turns most matrix derivatives into a few lines of algebra.</p>\n<p>Imagine you are tuning a recipe that has many ingredients arranged in a table. Instead of asking \"what happens if I change this one cell?\" for every cell separately, you ask \"if I change the whole table by a tiny amount <span class=\"m\">dW</span>, what is the tiny change in the result?\" The answer is always a linear expression in <span class=\"m\">dW</span>, and the gradient can be read straight off that expression.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-a-differential-is\" data-h=\"3\">What a differential is</h3>\n<p>For a function of one variable, the differential is <span class=\"m\">df = f'(x)\\,dx</span>: a small change in input times the slope. For a scalar function of a vector it is <span class=\"m\">df = \\nabla f^T d\\mathbf x = \\sum_i\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i}dx_i</span>. For a scalar function of a matrix it is\n<span class=\"mb\">dL = \\sum_{i,j}G_{ij}\\,dW_{ij} = \\operatorname{tr}(G^T dW),\\qquad G = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}</span>\nSo the whole game is: manipulate <span class=\"m\">dL</span> until it looks like <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)</span> for some matrix <span class=\"m\">M</span>. Then the gradient is <span class=\"m\">G = M^T</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Matrix differential</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The first-order change <span class=\"m\">dF</span> of a matrix (or vector, or scalar) expression <span class=\"m\">F</span> caused by a small change <span class=\"m\">dX</span> in its argument. It is linear in <span class=\"m\">dX</span>, and obeys the same sum and product rules as ordinary differentials.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-rules\" data-h=\"3\">The rules</h3>\n<p>Differentials obey rules you already know, with one caution: matrices do not commute, so you must keep the order of factors.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Constants: <span class=\"m\">dA = 0</span>.</li>\n<li>Sums: <span class=\"m\">d(X + Y) = dX + dY</span>.</li>\n<li>Products: <span class=\"m\">d(XY) = (dX)Y + X(dY)</span>. Keep <span class=\"m\">dX</span> in the place where <span class=\"m\">X</span> was.</li>\n<li>Transpose and trace: <span class=\"m\">d(X^T) = (dX)^T</span> and <span class=\"m\">d\\operatorname{tr}(X) = \\operatorname{tr}(dX)</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The product rule is the one people get wrong. In ordinary calculus <span class=\"m\">d(xy) = y\\,dx + x\\,dy</span> and the order does not matter. For matrices, writing <span class=\"m\">Y\\,dX</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">(dX)Y</span> is an error, because <span class=\"m\">Y\\,dX</span> and <span class=\"m\">(dX)Y</span> are usually different matrices.</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-facts-about-the-trace\" data-h=\"3\">Three facts about the trace</h3>\n<p>The trace (sum of diagonal entries) has three properties that make the method work. First, a scalar is its own trace: <span class=\"m\">a = \\operatorname{tr}(a)</span> for a <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span> matrix. Second, the trace is <strong>cyclic</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(CAB) = \\operatorname{tr}(BCA)</span>, as long as the shapes allow the products. Third, <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(A^T) = \\operatorname{tr}(A)</span>. Cyclic means you may rotate factors from the back to the front. It does <strong>not</strong> mean you may swap any two factors: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(ABC)</span> is generally not <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(BAC)</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-first-example\" data-h=\"3\">A first example</h3>\n<p>Take <span class=\"m\">L = \\mathbf a^TX\\mathbf b</span>. Differentiate: <span class=\"m\">dL = \\mathbf a^T(dX)\\mathbf b</span>. This is a scalar, so wrap it in a trace and rotate <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf b</span> to the front: <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf a^T\\,dX\\,\\mathbf b) = \\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf b\\mathbf a^T\\,dX)</span>. Now it has the form <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dX)</span> with <span class=\"m\">M = \\mathbf b\\mathbf a^T</span>, so the gradient is <span class=\"m\">M^T = \\mathbf a\\mathbf b^T</span>. That matches the identity from the previous lesson, with no indices at all.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-least-squares-gradient-for-a-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The least-squares gradient for a matrix</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">L = \\|W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y\\|^2 = \\mathbf r^T\\mathbf r</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">d\\mathbf r = (dW)\\mathbf x</span>, and <span class=\"m\">dL = 2\\mathbf r^Td\\mathbf r = 2\\mathbf r^T(dW)\\mathbf x = \\operatorname{tr}(2\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T\\,dW)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = (2\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T)^T = 2\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>. This is the outer product the shape rule predicted in the first lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-inverse\" data-h=\"3\">The inverse</h3>\n<p>Start from <span class=\"m\">XX^{-1} = I</span> and take differentials of both sides with the product rule: <span class=\"m\">(dX)X^{-1} + X\\,d(X^{-1}) = 0</span>. Solving,\n<span class=\"mb\">d(X^{-1}) = -X^{-1}(dX)X^{-1}</span>\nFor a <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span> matrix this says <span class=\"m\">d(1/x) = -dx/x^2</span>, the familiar derivative of <span class=\"m\">1/x</span>. With <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">dx = 0.01</span>, it predicts the change <span class=\"m\">-0.0025</span>, and indeed <span class=\"m\">1/2.01 - 1/2\\approx-0.00249</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-determinant\" data-h=\"3\">The determinant</h3>\n<p>The determinant measures volume scaling, and its differential is given by <strong>Jacobi's formula</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">d\\det X = \\det X\\cdot\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}dX)</span>\nReading off the gradient gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial\\det X}{\\partial X} = \\det X\\cdot X^{-T}</span>, and dividing by <span class=\"m\">\\det X</span> gives the very useful\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial\\log\\det X}{\\partial X} = X^{-T}</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">1\\times1</span> matrices that is the familiar rule <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d}{dx}\\ln x = \\frac1x</span>.</p>\n<p>Check with numbers. Let <span class=\"m\">X = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 3\\end{bmatrix}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\det X = 6</span>. Increase the top-left entry by <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>. The new determinant is <span class=\"m\">2.1\\times3 = 6.3</span>, a change of <span class=\"m\">0.3</span>. Jacobi's formula gives <span class=\"m\">6\\cdot\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(X^{-1}dX\\right)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">X^{-1}dX</span> has only one nonzero entry, <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\times0.1 = 0.05</span>, on its diagonal. So the predicted change is <span class=\"m\">6\\times0.05 = 0.3</span>. Exactly right.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key idea</div><div class=\"box-title\">The recipe</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Write the differential <span class=\"m\">dL</span> using the sum and product rules, keeping factors in order.</li>\n<li>Wrap the scalar <span class=\"m\">dL</span> in a trace.</li>\n<li>Use the cyclic property to move <span class=\"m\">dW</span> to the far right: <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)</span>.</li>\n<li>Read off <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = M^T</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Swapping the order of factors in the product rule. Forgetting the final transpose when reading off the gradient (the gradient is <span class=\"m\">M^T</span>, not <span class=\"m\">M</span>). Treating the trace as fully commutative: you may rotate factors cyclically, but not swap two of them at will. Writing <span class=\"m\">d(X^{-1}) = -X^{-2}dX</span>, which is only true when <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">dX</span> commute.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-this-is-worth-learning\" data-h=\"3\">Why this is worth learning</h3>\n<p>The differential method scales. A derivation that would take a page of indices becomes a few lines, and each line is a rule you can check. It also mirrors how automatic differentiation libraries are written: each operation, such as a matrix product, an inverse or a determinant, comes with a rule describing how a small change in its inputs produces a small change in its output. When you understand the rules here, you can read the source code of those libraries, and you can derive the gradient of a new custom layer before trusting a framework to do it for you.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A differential is the linear first-order change of an expression. Products follow the ordered product rule, inverses obey <span class=\"m\">d(X^{-1}) = -X^{-1}dX\\,X^{-1}</span>, determinants obey Jacobi's formula, and <span class=\"m\">\\log\\det X</span> has gradient <span class=\"m\">X^{-T}</span>. To get any gradient, write <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)</span> and transpose <span class=\"m\">M</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;scale&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The yellow square's area is <span class=\"m\">|\\det A|</span>. Drag one column handle a little and watch how the determinant responds.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Pick the scale preset, note <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span>, then drag the pink handle a small distance to the right (increasing the top-left entry <span class=\"m\">a</span>).\n2. Predict the change before you look: for <span class=\"m\">A = \\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\\\c &amp; d\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial\\det A}{\\partial a} = d</span>, the vertical height of the blue column.\n3. Drag the blue handle upward instead, increasing <span class=\"m\">d</span>; the change in <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span> should be about <span class=\"m\">a</span> times the step.\n4. Make the two columns nearly parallel and watch <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span> head to 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the sensitivity of the area to each entry is exactly the matrix <span class=\"m\">\\det A\\cdot A^{-T} = \\begin{bmatrix}d &amp; -c\\\\-b &amp; a\\end{bmatrix}</span> from Jacobi's formula. Near a singular matrix the determinant is small, so <span class=\"m\">\\log\\det A</span> changes very fast, which is why <span class=\"m\">X^{-T}</span> blows up there.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(G^TdW)\\iff G = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W};\\qquad dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = M^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(XY) = (dX)Y + X(dY)\\qquad d(X^T) = (dX)^T\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(CAB)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(X^{-1}) = -X^{-1}(dX)X^{-1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d\\det X = \\det X\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}dX)\\qquad \\frac{\\partial\\log\\det X}{\\partial X} = X^{-T}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(X^TAX) = (A + A^T)X\\qquad\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(AX^{-1}) = -X^{-T}A^TX^{-T}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reading off the gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)</span> for every small <span class=\"m\">dW</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = M^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW) = \\sum_i(M\\,dW)_{ii} = \\sum_i\\sum_jM_{ij}dW_{ji}</span>. The coefficient of <span class=\"m\">dW_{ji}</span> is <span class=\"m\">M_{ij}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ji}} = M_{ij} = (M^T)_{ji}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobi's formula</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For invertible <span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">d\\det X = \\det X\\cdot\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}dX)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det(X + dX) = \\det X\\cdot\\det(I + X^{-1}dX)</span>. For a small matrix <span class=\"m\">E</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\det(I + E) = \\prod_i(1 + \\lambda_i(E))\\approx1 + \\sum_i\\lambda_i(E) = 1 + \\operatorname{tr}E</span> to first order. With <span class=\"m\">E = X^{-1}dX</span>, subtracting <span class=\"m\">\\det X</span> leaves <span class=\"m\">\\det X\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}dX)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(X^TAX)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Product rule: <span class=\"m\">d\\operatorname{tr}(X^TAX) = \\operatorname{tr}((dX)^TAX) + \\operatorname{tr}(X^TA\\,dX)</span>.</li>\n<li>A trace equals the trace of its transpose: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}((dX)^TAX) = \\operatorname{tr}(X^TA^TdX)</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(X^T(A^T + A)\\,dX)</span>, with <span class=\"m\">M = X^T(A + A^T)</span>.</li>\n<li>Gradient: <span class=\"m\">M^T = (A + A^T)X</span>. For a single column <span class=\"m\">X = \\mathbf x</span> this is the quadratic-form identity.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Check <span class=\"m\">d(X^{-1})</span> on <span class=\"m\">X = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> when <span class=\"m\">X_{11}</span> grows by <span class=\"m\">0.1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X^{-1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0.5 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 0.25\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">dX</span> has a single entry <span class=\"m\">0.1</span> in position <span class=\"m\">(1,1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">-X^{-1}dX\\,X^{-1}</span> has a single entry <span class=\"m\">-0.5\\times0.1\\times0.5 = -0.025</span> in position <span class=\"m\">(1,1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Exact: <span class=\"m\">1/2.1 - 1/2 = -0.0238</span>. The first-order prediction is close, and gets closer for smaller steps.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient of the Gaussian log-likelihood term <span class=\"m\">-\\frac12\\log\\det\\Sigma</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">d\\left(-\\tfrac12\\log\\det\\Sigma\\right) = -\\tfrac12\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma^{-1}d\\Sigma)</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">M = -\\frac12\\Sigma^{-1}</span> and the gradient is <span class=\"m\">-\\frac12\\Sigma^{-T} = -\\frac12\\Sigma^{-1}</span>, since a covariance matrix is symmetric.</li>\n<li>Combined with the gradient of the data term, setting the total to zero gives the familiar estimate <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\Sigma = \\frac1n\\sum_i(\\mathbf x_i - \\boldsymbol\\mu)(\\mathbf x_i - \\boldsymbol\\mu)^T</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">L(W) = \\|W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">W = I</span> (<span class=\"m\">2\\times2</span>), <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (1, 1)</span>. (a) Use differentials to show <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = 2(W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y)\\mathbf x^T</span>. (b) Evaluate it. (c) Predict the change in <span class=\"m\">L</span> if <span class=\"m\">W_{22}</span> decreases by <span class=\"m\">0.05</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">dL = 2\\mathbf r^T(dW)\\mathbf x = \\operatorname{tr}(2\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^TdW)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = W\\mathbf x - \\mathbf y</span>, so the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2\\mathbf x\\mathbf r^T)^T = 2\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = (1, 2) - (1, 1) = (0, 1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">2\\mathbf r\\mathbf x^T = \\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">\\Delta L\\approx4\\times(-0.05) = -0.2</span>. Exact: <span class=\"m\">L</span> goes from <span class=\"m\">1</span> to <span class=\"m\">(1.9 - 1)^2 = 0.81</span>, a change of <span class=\"m\">-0.19</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Fitting a multivariate Gaussian, Gaussian processes, and variational inference all need <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial\\Sigma}\\log\\det\\Sigma = \\Sigma^{-1}</span> and the inverse rule. Normalizing flows need gradients of <span class=\"m\">\\log|\\det J|</span>. Autodiff libraries implement exactly these differential rules for <code>inv</code>, <code>det</code>, <code>slogdet</code> and <code>solve</code>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Numerically, never form <span class=\"m\">X^{-1}</span> just to use it once. Compute <span class=\"m\">X^{-1}\\mathbf b</span> with a linear solve and <span class=\"m\">\\log\\det X</span> from a Cholesky or LU factorization (it is the sum of the logs of the diagonal factors). These are faster and far more accurate.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Economists use the Leontief input-output model, where total production is <span class=\"m\">(I - A)^{-1}\\mathbf d</span> for an industry-requirements matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span> and demand <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf d</span>. The inverse rule <span class=\"m\">d(I - A)^{-1} = (I - A)^{-1}(dA)(I - A)^{-1}</span> tells planners how total output across the economy shifts when one industry becomes more efficient.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which equality always holds when the products are defined?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(CAB)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(BAC)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(AB) = \\operatorname{tr}(A)\\operatorname{tr}(B)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The trace is invariant under cyclic rotations only.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">dW</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">M</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">M^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">M^{-1}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW) = \\sum_{i,j}M_{ij}dW_{ji}</span>, so the coefficient of <span class=\"m\">dW_{ji}</span> is <span class=\"m\">M_{ij}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which is the correct differential of a product of matrices?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">d(XY) = (dX)Y + X(dY)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">d(XY) = Y(dX) + X(dY)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">d(XY) = (dX)(dY)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Keep each factor in its original position; matrices do not commute.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">X = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\0 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, what is the <span class=\"m\">(1,1)</span> entry of <span class=\"m\">\\partial\\log\\det X/\\partial X</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.5|1/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The gradient is <span class=\"m\">X^{-T}</span>, whose <span class=\"m\">(1,1)</span> entry is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">X = \\begin{bmatrix}3 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 5\\end{bmatrix}</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\partial\\det X/\\partial X_{11}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det X = X_{11}X_{22} - X_{12}X_{21}</span>, so the derivative with respect to <span class=\"m\">X_{11}</span> is <span class=\"m\">X_{22} = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using <span class=\"m\">d(1/x) = -dx/x^2</span>, estimate the change in <span class=\"m\">1/x</span> when <span class=\"m\">x</span> goes from 4 to 4.1.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-0.00625|-1/160\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-0.1/16 = -0.00625</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "vector-chain": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"from-one-chain-to-many\" data-h=\"3\">From one chain to many</h3>\n<p>In single-variable calculus the chain rule says that if <span class=\"m\">y = g(x)</span> and <span class=\"m\">z = f(y)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dx} = \\frac{dz}{dy}\\cdot\\frac{dy}{dx}</span>. Rates of change multiply. If converting euros to dollars multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1.1</span> and converting dollars to yen multiplies by <span class=\"m\">150</span>, converting euros to yen multiplies by <span class=\"m\">1.1\\times150 = 165</span>.</p>\n<p>A neural network is a long chain of such conversions, except that each stage turns a whole vector into another vector. The question of this lesson is: what replaces \"multiply the rates\" when every stage has many inputs and many outputs? The answer is beautifully simple. Each stage is locally a linear map, its Jacobian. Doing one linear map after another is the same as multiplying their matrices. So <strong>Jacobians multiply</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rule\" data-h=\"3\">The rule</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = g(\\mathbf x)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = f(\\mathbf y)</span>, then\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{f\\circ g}(\\mathbf x) = J_f(g(\\mathbf x))\\,J_g(\\mathbf x)</span>\nThe order matters: the Jacobian of the <strong>last</strong> function goes on the <strong>left</strong>, just as in the composition <span class=\"m\">f(g(\\mathbf x))</span>. And <span class=\"m\">J_f</span> is evaluated at the intermediate point <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = g(\\mathbf x)</span>, not at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>. The shapes always fit: if <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb{R}^m</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z\\in\\mathbb{R}^k</span>, then <span class=\"m\">J_f</span> is <span class=\"m\">k\\times m</span>, <span class=\"m\">J_g</span> is <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span>, and the product is <span class=\"m\">k\\times n</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-numerical-example\" data-h=\"3\">A numerical example</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">g(x_1, x_2) = (x_1 + x_2,\\ x_1x_2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(y_1, y_2) = y_1^2 + y_2</span>. At <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 2)</span> the intermediate point is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (3, 2)</span>. The Jacobians are <span class=\"m\">J_g = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\x_2 &amp; x_1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">J_f = \\begin{bmatrix}2y_1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Multiplying, <span class=\"m\">J_fJ_g = \\begin{bmatrix}6 + 2 &amp; 6 + 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}8 &amp; 7\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<p>Check it directly. The composite is <span class=\"m\">h = (x_1 + x_2)^2 + x_1x_2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x_1} = 2(x_1 + x_2) + x_2 = 8</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x_2} = 2(x_1 + x_2) + x_1 = 7</span>. Same answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"scalar-losses-and-the-transpose\" data-h=\"3\">Scalar losses and the transpose</h3>\n<p>In machine learning the very last function is a scalar loss <span class=\"m\">L</span>. Its Jacobian is a <span class=\"m\">1\\times m</span> row, and the gradient is the column version. Transposing the chain rule <span class=\"m\">J_{L\\circ g} = J_LJ_g</span> gives the form we will use constantly:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}L = J_g^T\\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf y}L</span>\nIn words: to turn the gradient with respect to a layer's <strong>output</strong> into the gradient with respect to its <strong>input</strong>, multiply by the transposed Jacobian of that layer. We write <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = \\nabla_{\\mathbf y}L</span> for this \"upstream gradient\" and call <span class=\"m\">J^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span> a <strong>vector-Jacobian product</strong>, or VJP.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Vector-Jacobian product (VJP)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a function <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = g(\\mathbf x)</span> and an upstream gradient <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>, the vector <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = J_g^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>. It is the gradient of <span class=\"m\">L</span> with respect to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>, computed without ever forming <span class=\"m\">J_g</span> when <span class=\"m\">g</span> has structure.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-the-order-of-multiplication-matters\" data-h=\"3\">Why the order of multiplication matters</h3>\n<p>Suppose a network has three layers, each with a <span class=\"m\">1000\\times1000</span> Jacobian, followed by a scalar loss. The gradient is <span class=\"m\">J_1^TJ_2^TJ_3^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}_3</span>. You could multiply the matrices first, which costs about <span class=\"m\">10^9</span> multiply-adds per matrix-matrix product. Or you could start from the vector on the right, so that every step is a matrix times a <strong>vector</strong>, costing about <span class=\"m\">10^6</span> each. That is a thousand times cheaper. Backpropagation is exactly this choice: start at the scalar loss and pass a vector backwards.</p>\n<h3 id=\"vjps-for-the-layers-you-will-meet\" data-h=\"3\">VJPs for the layers you will meet</h3>\n<p>The best part is that common layers have VJPs that are even cheaper than a matrix-vector product with a full Jacobian.</p>\n<p><strong>Linear layer</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x</span>. The Jacobian with respect to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> is <span class=\"m\">W</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>. The gradient for the weights is <span class=\"m\">\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>, an outer product. For <span class=\"m\">W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = (1, -1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = (1 - 3,\\ 2 - 4) = (-2, -2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 1\\\\-1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Elementwise function</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = \\sigma(\\mathbf x)</span>, applied to each entry. Output <span class=\"m\">i</span> depends only on input <span class=\"m\">i</span>, so the Jacobian is diagonal with entries <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(x_i)</span>. Multiplying by a diagonal matrix is just multiplying entry by entry: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf x)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\odot</span> is the elementwise (Hadamard) product. For ReLU, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span> is 1 where the input was positive and 0 elsewhere, so the gradient simply passes through the active units and is blocked at the inactive ones.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-paths-split-and-merge\" data-h=\"3\">When paths split and merge</h3>\n<p>Sometimes one variable feeds into several places. Then the gradients from every path <strong>add</strong>. If <span class=\"m\">L = u + v</span> with <span class=\"m\">u = 2x</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = x^2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dL}{dx} = 2 + 2x</span>: one contribution through <span class=\"m\">u</span>, one through <span class=\"m\">v</span>. In a network this happens with shared weights and with residual connections, and autodiff systems handle it by accumulating (summing) gradients.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Multiplying Jacobians in the wrong order (<span class=\"m\">J_gJ_f</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">J_fJ_g</span>). Evaluating <span class=\"m\">J_f</span> at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> instead of at <span class=\"m\">g(\\mathbf x)</span>. Building a full diagonal Jacobian for an elementwise function instead of multiplying elementwise. Forgetting to add the contributions when a variable is used more than once.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-two-layer-picture-in-words\" data-h=\"3\">A two-layer picture in words</h3>\n<p>It helps to tell the whole story once without symbols. Picture a small network that takes an input vector, multiplies it by a weight matrix, squashes each entry with a nonlinearity, multiplies by a second weight matrix and finally scores the result with a loss. On the forward pass, numbers flow left to right and each stage remembers what it received. On the backward pass, a single vector flows right to left. It starts as the gradient of the loss with respect to the final output. At the second weight matrix it is multiplied by that matrix's transpose, which carries it back into the space of hidden units. At the nonlinearity it is multiplied entry by entry by the slopes of the squashing function at the values remembered from the forward pass. At the first weight matrix it is multiplied by that transpose, which carries it back to the input. Along the way, each weight matrix receives its own gradient as an outer product of the vector arriving from the right and the input it remembered from the left.</p>\n<p>Every one of those steps is a vector-Jacobian product, and the whole backward pass costs about as much as the forward pass. Nothing more is going on inside the largest language models, only with many more layers and much bigger matrices. Once you are comfortable with the chain rule as a product of local linear maps, backpropagation is simply bookkeeping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Composing functions multiplies their Jacobians, last function on the left. For a scalar loss, gradients travel backwards as <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = J^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>. Linear layers give <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>, elementwise layers give <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf x)</span>, and gradients from separate paths add.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-jacobian\" data-args='{&quot;map&quot;: &quot;tanh&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The map is a tiny layer <span class=\"m\">\\tanh(W\\mathbf v)</span>. Its Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{diag}(1 - \\tanh^2(W\\mathbf v))\\,W</span>, a product of two Jacobians.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the point to the origin. Since <span class=\"m\">\\tanh'(0) = 1</span>, the panel should show <span class=\"m\">J = W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0.5\\\\-0.5 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>.\n2. Drag the point far to the upper right, say <span class=\"m\">(2, 1.5)</span>, and watch the entries of the first row shrink.\n3. Shrink the square and confirm that the orange (linear) and blue (true) grids agree near any point.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the chain rule says <span class=\"m\">J = D\\,W</span> where <span class=\"m\">D</span> is the diagonal Jacobian of tanh. Near the origin <span class=\"m\">D\\approx I</span>, so the layer acts like <span class=\"m\">W</span>. Where tanh saturates, <span class=\"m\">D</span> has entries close to 0, the whole row of <span class=\"m\">J</span> is squashed, and the output barely responds. Gradients flowing back through <span class=\"m\">J^T</span> get squashed the same way. Stack many such layers and you get the vanishing-gradient problem.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">J_{f\\circ g}(\\mathbf x) = J_f(g(\\mathbf x))\\,J_g(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}L = J_g^T\\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf y}L\\qquad(\\bar{\\mathbf x} = J^T\\bar{\\mathbf y})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x:\\quad\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y},\\quad\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf y = \\sigma(\\mathbf x)\\ \\text{elementwise}:\\quad\\bar{\\mathbf x} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf x)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chain rule for Jacobians</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">g</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">g(\\mathbf x)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">f\\circ g</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> and <span class=\"m\">J_{f\\circ g}(\\mathbf x) = J_f(g(\\mathbf x))J_g(\\mathbf x)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Linearize each map: <span class=\"m\">g(\\mathbf x + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx g(\\mathbf x) + J_g\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf y + \\boldsymbol\\delta)\\approx f(\\mathbf y) + J_f\\boldsymbol\\delta</span>. Substituting <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta = J_g\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span> gives <span class=\"m\">f(g(\\mathbf x + \\boldsymbol\\Delta))\\approx f(g(\\mathbf x)) + J_fJ_g\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>. The error terms are smaller than <span class=\"m\">\\|\\boldsymbol\\Delta\\|</span>, so <span class=\"m\">J_fJ_g</span> is the derivative.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">VJP of a linear layer</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x</span> and scalar <span class=\"m\">L</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = \\nabla_{\\mathbf y}L</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}L = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">dL = \\bar{\\mathbf y}^Td\\mathbf y</span> and <span class=\"m\">d\\mathbf y = (dW)\\mathbf x + W\\,d\\mathbf x</span>. The <span class=\"m\">d\\mathbf x</span> part is <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}^TW\\,d\\mathbf x = (W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y})^Td\\mathbf x</span>. The <span class=\"m\">dW</span> part is <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}^T(dW)\\mathbf x = \\operatorname{tr}(\\mathbf x\\bar{\\mathbf y}^TdW)</span>, whose gradient is <span class=\"m\">(\\mathbf x\\bar{\\mathbf y}^T)^T = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Backward through <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = \\operatorname{ReLU}(W\\mathbf x)</span> with <span class=\"m\">W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\-1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = (1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forward: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = W\\mathbf x = (4, -1)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (4, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li>ReLU VJP: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf z} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\odot\\mathbb 1[\\mathbf z &gt; 0] = (1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li>Linear VJP: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf z} = (1, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Weight gradient: <span class=\"m\">\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf z}\\mathbf x^T = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 0\\end{bmatrix}</span>. The inactive unit's row gets no gradient.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count the cost: three <span class=\"m\">1000\\times1000</span> Jacobians and a scalar loss</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Matrix first: <span class=\"m\">J_1^TJ_2^T</span> costs about <span class=\"m\">1000^3 = 10^9</span> multiply-adds, and so does the next product.</li>\n<li>Vector first: <span class=\"m\">J_3^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>, then <span class=\"m\">J_2^T(\\cdot)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">J_1^T(\\cdot)</span>, each about <span class=\"m\">10^6</span>.</li>\n<li>Total: about <span class=\"m\">2\\times10^9</span> versus <span class=\"m\">3\\times10^6</span>, roughly 700 times cheaper. That is why backprop works from the loss backwards.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x</span> and <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12\\|\\mathbf y - \\mathbf t\\|^2</span>, with <span class=\"m\">W = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf t = (1, 1)</span>. Find <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar W</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x}</span>, and check one entry of <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x}</span> directly.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Forward: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (2, 3)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = \\mathbf y - \\mathbf t = (1, 2)</span>. Weight gradient <span class=\"m\">\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\2 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Input gradient <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\0 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix} = (4, 2)</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12[(2x_1 - 1)^2 + (x_1 + x_2 - 1)^2]</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial x_2} = x_1 + x_2 - 1 = 2</span>. Correct.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every operation in PyTorch or JAX registers a VJP rule (PyTorch calls it <code>backward</code>, JAX calls it <code>vjp</code>). The framework only ever multiplies upstream gradients by these rules; it never builds a full Jacobian. Forward-mode products <span class=\"m\">J\\mathbf v</span> (JVPs) are also available and are used for things like Hessian-vector products.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Choosing where to start multiplying a chain of matrices is the classic <strong>matrix-chain multiplication</strong> problem, solved by dynamic programming. Reverse-mode autodiff is the special case where the chain ends in a <span class=\"m\">1\\times m</span> row, so starting from that end is always optimal.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A bicycle's gears are a chain rule. The pedal turns the front gear, which turns the chain, which turns the rear gear, which turns the wheel. The wheel's speed per pedal turn is the product of the gear ratios along the way, exactly like multiplying derivatives.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">h = f\\circ g</span>, the Jacobian <span class=\"m\">J_h(\\mathbf x)</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">J_f(g(\\mathbf x))\\,J_g(\\mathbf x)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">J_g(\\mathbf x)\\,J_f(g(\\mathbf x))</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">J_f(\\mathbf x)\\,J_g(\\mathbf x)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Last function on the left, evaluated at the intermediate point.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Let <span class=\"m\">g(x) = (x, x^2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(y_1, y_2) = y_1y_2</span>. Using the chain rule, what is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d}{dx}f(g(x))</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">J_f = [y_2, y_1] = [4, 2]</span> and <span class=\"m\">J_g = (1, 2x) = (1, 4)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">4\\cdot1 + 2\\cdot4 = 12</span>. Directly, <span class=\"m\">f(g(x)) = x^3</span> with derivative <span class=\"m\">3x^2 = 12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x</span> with upstream gradient <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>, the gradient for <span class=\"m\">W</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\bar{\\mathbf y}^T</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Shape check: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span> is <span class=\"m\">m\\times1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x^T</span> is <span class=\"m\">1\\times n</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> like <span class=\"m\">W</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The Jacobian of an elementwise function like ReLU applied to a vector is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">diagonal</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">all ones</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">symmetric but dense</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Output <span class=\"m\">i</span> depends only on input <span class=\"m\">i</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">L = u + v</span> where <span class=\"m\">u = 3x</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = x^2</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">dL/dx</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The two paths add: <span class=\"m\">3 + 2x = 5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">W = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\\\3 &amp; 4\\end{bmatrix}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf y} = (1, 0)</span>. What is the second entry of <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y}</span> is the first row of <span class=\"m\">W</span> written as a column: <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "autodiff": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-receipt-you-can-read-backwards\" data-h=\"3\">A receipt you can read backwards</h3>\n<p>Imagine a café bill. The coffee costs 3 euros, you buy 2, a 1 euro service fee is added, and then a 4 euro voucher is subtracted. Every step is simple: a multiplication, an addition, a subtraction. If the café owner asks \"how much does the final bill change if the coffee price goes up by one cent?\", you could recompute the whole bill, but you could also walk back through the steps: the voucher and the fee do not depend on the price, and the multiplication by 2 doubles any change. So the bill rises by two cents.</p>\n<p>Computers differentiate in exactly this way. They do not manipulate formulas symbolically, and they do not guess with finite differences. They record the sequence of elementary operations that produced the answer and apply the chain rule one step at a time. This technique is called <strong>automatic differentiation</strong>, or autodiff, and it is the engine inside every deep learning library.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-computational-graph\" data-h=\"3\">The computational graph</h3>\n<p>Take the loss <span class=\"m\">L = (wx + b - y)^2</span> of a one-weight linear model. Break it into elementary steps, each with a name:\n<span class=\"mb\">u = wx,\\qquad v = u + b,\\qquad e = v - y,\\qquad L = e^2</span>\nDrawn as a picture, each step is a node and arrows show which values feed which. This picture is the <strong>computational graph</strong>. With <span class=\"m\">w = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 4</span>, the forward pass gives <span class=\"m\">u = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">e = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">L = 9</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Computational graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A directed acyclic graph whose nodes are elementary operations (add, multiply, exp, ReLU, ...) and whose edges carry intermediate values from one operation to the next. Evaluating the nodes in order is the forward pass.</p></div></div>\n<p>The important point is that every node is simple, and simple operations have derivatives we know. A multiply node <span class=\"m\">u = wx</span> has local derivatives <span class=\"m\">\\partial u/\\partial w = x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\partial u/\\partial x = w</span>. An add node has local derivatives 1 and 1. A subtract node has <span class=\"m\">1</span> and <span class=\"m\">-1</span>. A square node has <span class=\"m\">2e</span>. That is all the calculus we need.</p>\n<h3 id=\"forward-mode-carry-the-derivative-along\" data-h=\"3\">Forward mode: carry the derivative along</h3>\n<p>One way to differentiate is to carry, next to every value, its derivative with respect to one chosen input. Suppose we want <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial w</span>. Seed <span class=\"m\">\\dot w = 1</span> (and <span class=\"m\">\\dot x = \\dot b = \\dot y = 0</span>). Then apply the local rules in the forward direction: <span class=\"m\">\\dot u = x\\dot w + w\\dot x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\dot v = \\dot u + \\dot b = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\dot e = \\dot v - \\dot y = 2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\dot L = 2e\\,\\dot e = 2\\cdot3\\cdot2 = 12</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial w = 12</span>.</p>\n<p>This is <strong>forward-mode</strong> autodiff. It is simple and needs no extra memory, but it gives the derivative with respect to only one input per pass. A network with a million weights would need a million passes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reverse-mode-pull-the-derivative-back\" data-h=\"3\">Reverse mode: pull the derivative back</h3>\n<p>The alternative runs the chain rule from the output backwards. For every value <span class=\"m\">v</span> define its <strong>adjoint</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = \\partial L/\\partial v</span>, the sensitivity of the final loss to that value. Start with <span class=\"m\">\\bar L = 1</span> and walk the graph in reverse:</p>\n<p>The square node gives <span class=\"m\">\\bar e = 2e\\cdot\\bar L = 6</span>. The subtract node passes the adjoint on: <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = \\bar e = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar y = -\\bar e = -6</span>. The add node copies it to both inputs: <span class=\"m\">\\bar u = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b = 6</span>. The multiply node swaps its inputs: <span class=\"m\">\\bar w = \\bar u\\cdot x = 12</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar x = \\bar u\\cdot w = 18</span>.</p>\n<p>One backward pass produced the derivative with respect to <strong>every</strong> input at once. We can check one: <span class=\"m\">L = (2w + 1 - 4)^2 = (2w - 3)^2</span> as a function of <span class=\"m\">w</span>, so <span class=\"m\">dL/dw = 4(2w - 3) = 12</span> at <span class=\"m\">w = 3</span>. Correct.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reverse-mode automatic differentiation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Computing the derivatives of one scalar output with respect to all inputs by one backward sweep over the computational graph, propagating adjoints <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = \\partial L/\\partial v</span>. Its cost is a small constant times the cost of the forward pass. Backpropagation is reverse mode applied to neural networks.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"three-patterns-that-cover-everything\" data-h=\"3\">Three patterns that cover everything</h3>\n<p>Watching the backward pass, three patterns appear again and again. An <strong>add</strong> node distributes its adjoint unchanged to all its inputs. A <strong>multiply</strong> node sends each input the adjoint times the <strong>other</strong> input. And when one value is used in several places (a <strong>fan-out</strong>), its adjoint is the <strong>sum</strong> of the adjoints arriving from each use. For example, for <span class=\"m\">f = x\\cdot x</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>, each of the two uses sends back <span class=\"m\">\\bar f\\cdot x = 3</span>, and the total is <span class=\"m\">6 = 2x</span>, as it should be.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-reverse-mode-wins-for-machine-learning\" data-h=\"3\">Why reverse mode wins for machine learning</h3>\n<p>A loss is one number that depends on many parameters. Forward mode needs one pass per parameter; reverse mode needs one pass in total. The price of reverse mode is memory: the backward pass needs the intermediate values from the forward pass (we used <span class=\"m\">x</span>, <span class=\"m\">w</span> and <span class=\"m\">e</span> above), so they must be stored. For deep networks this memory for stored activations is often the limiting factor on batch size.</p>\n<h3 id=\"checking-gradients-numerically\" data-h=\"3\">Checking gradients numerically</h3>\n<p>Hand-derived or newly implemented gradients should always be checked against a <strong>central difference</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w}\\approx\\frac{L(w + h) - L(w - h)}{2h}</span>\nFor our example with <span class=\"m\">h = 0.01</span>: <span class=\"m\">L(3.01) = 3.02^2 = 9.1204</span> and <span class=\"m\">L(2.99) = 2.98^2 = 8.8804</span>, so the estimate is <span class=\"m\">0.24/0.02 = 12</span>. The central difference has error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>, while the one-sided version <span class=\"m\">(L(w + h) - L(w))/h</span> has error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h</span>, so the central form is far more accurate. Use it for testing only; it costs two forward passes per parameter.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Overwriting an adjoint instead of adding to it when a value is used twice. Forgetting that a subtraction sends a negative adjoint to its second input. Using a step <span class=\"m\">h</span> so tiny (like <span class=\"m\">10^{-12}</span>) that rounding error swamps the difference; around <span class=\"m\">10^{-5}</span> is a good choice in double precision. Treating finite differences as a training method: they are a check, not a replacement.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A computational graph breaks a calculation into elementary steps with known local derivatives. Forward mode carries one derivative forward per pass. Reverse mode carries adjoints backwards and gets every partial derivative of a scalar output in one pass, at the cost of storing intermediate values. Add nodes copy adjoints, multiply nodes swap inputs, and fan-outs sum. Always confirm gradients with a central difference.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-compgraph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;linear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The graph of <span class=\"m\">L = (wx + b - y)^2</span>. Values flow forward; press Backward step to send adjoints back one node at a time.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the defaults <span class=\"m\">w = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 4</span>, read the forward values: <span class=\"m\">u = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">e = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = 9</span>.\n2. Press Backward step repeatedly and predict each adjoint before it appears: <span class=\"m\">\\bar e = 6</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = 6</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\bar u = \\bar b = 6</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\bar w = 12</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar x = 18</span>.\n3. Compare <span class=\"m\">\\bar w</span> with the numerical central-difference estimate shown below the graph.\n4. Change <span class=\"m\">b</span> until <span class=\"m\">e = 0</span> (try <span class=\"m\">b = -2</span>). All adjoints become 0: at the minimum, nothing needs to change.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each step uses only the local rule of one node and the adjoint arriving from its right, exactly the three patterns in the article. The multiply node really does swap: <span class=\"m\">\\bar w</span> is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar x</span> by <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar v = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v},\\qquad\\bar L = 1,\\qquad\\bar v = \\sum_{c\\,\\in\\,\\text{children}(v)}\\bar c\\,\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c = a + b:\\ \\bar a\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c,\\ \\bar b\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\qquad c = ab:\\ \\bar a\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\,b,\\ \\bar b\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\,a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{forward mode: }\\dot c = \\sum_{p\\,\\in\\,\\text{parents}(c)}\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial p}\\dot p</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{L(w + h) - L(w - h)}{2h} = \\frac{dL}{dw} + O(h^2)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cheap gradient principle</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If a scalar function <span class=\"m\">f</span> is evaluated by a program of elementary operations, reverse-mode autodiff computes the full gradient <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> with a cost that is at most a small constant (around 3 to 5) times the cost of evaluating <span class=\"m\">f</span>, independent of the number of inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sketch</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Each elementary operation has a fixed number of inputs, so its backward rule does a constant amount of work: a few multiplications and additions into the adjoints of its inputs. The backward pass visits every operation once, so its total cost is a constant times the number of operations, which is the cost of the forward pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Accuracy of the central difference</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L</span> has a continuous third derivative, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{L(w + h) - L(w - h)}{2h} = L'(w) + O(h^2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By Taylor's theorem, <span class=\"m\">L(w\\pm h) = L(w)\\pm hL'(w) + \\frac{h^2}2L''(w)\\pm\\frac{h^3}6L'''(\\xi_\\pm)</span>. Subtracting cancels the even terms, leaving <span class=\"m\">2hL'(w) + O(h^3)</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">2h</span> gives the result.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Forward mode for <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial x</span> in <span class=\"m\">L = (wx + b - y)^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">w = 3, x = 2, b = 1, y = 4</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Seed <span class=\"m\">\\dot x = 1</span> and all other inputs 0.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\dot u = w\\dot x = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\dot v = \\dot u = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\dot e = \\dot v = 3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\dot L = 2e\\,\\dot e = 2\\cdot3\\cdot3 = 18</span>, matching <span class=\"m\">\\bar x = 18</span> from reverse mode.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reverse mode with a fan-out: <span class=\"m\">f = (x + y)\\,y</span> at <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forward: <span class=\"m\">a = x + y = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">f = a\\,y = 6</span>.</li>\n<li>Backward from <span class=\"m\">\\bar f = 1</span>. Multiply node: <span class=\"m\">\\bar a = \\bar f\\,y = 2</span> and the direct contribution to <span class=\"m\">\\bar y</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\bar f\\,a = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Add node: <span class=\"m\">\\bar x = \\bar a = 2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">y</span> receives another <span class=\"m\">\\bar a = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Sum the two uses of <span class=\"m\">y</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\bar y = 3 + 2 = 5</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">f = xy + y^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\partial f/\\partial y = x + 2y = 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">L = (wx + b - y)^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">w = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>: (a) run the forward pass; (b) run the backward pass to get <span class=\"m\">\\bar w</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar b</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar y</span>; (c) a model has one million parameters and a scalar loss. How many reverse passes and how many forward-mode passes are needed for the full gradient?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">u = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">e = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = 1</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">\\bar e = 2e = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar y = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar b = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar u = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar w = \\bar u\\,x = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar x = \\bar u\\,w = 4</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">dL/dw = 2(wx + b - y)x = 2\\cdot1\\cdot(-1) = -2</span>.\n(c) One reverse pass, versus one million forward-mode passes.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PyTorch records a graph as your code runs and <code>loss.backward()</code> performs reverse mode over it; JAX's <code>grad</code> does the same by tracing your function. Because activations must be stored for the backward pass, large models use <strong>gradient checkpointing</strong>: store only some activations and recompute the rest during the backward pass, trading extra compute for memory.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Forward mode can be implemented with <strong>dual numbers</strong> <span class=\"m\">a + b\\varepsilon</span> where <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon^2 = 0</span>: evaluating <span class=\"m\">f(x + \\varepsilon)</span> yields <span class=\"m\">f(x) + f'(x)\\varepsilon</span>. Reverse mode uses a <strong>tape</strong>: a list of operations recorded in topological order and then replayed backwards. It is a good exercise in graph algorithms.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Spreadsheet auditing works like reverse mode. An accountant who wants to know which input cells most affect the final profit figure traces the formula dependencies backwards from that one cell, and the influence of each input is the product of sensitivities along the paths, summed over paths.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">L = (wx)^2</span> with <span class=\"m\">w = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial w</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"36\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2(wx)\\cdot x = 2\\cdot6\\cdot3 = 36</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For a scalar loss of many parameters, which mode is efficient?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Reverse mode</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Forward mode</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Finite differences</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Reverse mode gets all partial derivatives in one backward pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Node <span class=\"m\">z = x + y</span> receives adjoint <span class=\"m\">\\bar z = 5</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\bar x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>An add node passes its adjoint unchanged to each input.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Node <span class=\"m\">z = xy</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 7</span> receives <span class=\"m\">\\bar z = 1</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\bar x</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A multiply node sends each input the adjoint times the other input.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x\\cdot x</span> computed as a multiply node with both inputs equal to <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span>, what total adjoint does <span class=\"m\">x</span> receive?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each use contributes <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> and fan-outs sum, giving <span class=\"m\">2x = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The error of the central difference <span class=\"m\">\\frac{L(w + h) - L(w - h)}{2h}</span> shrinks like...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">h</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">h^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">h^3</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Taylor expansion cancels the <span class=\"m\">h^2</span> term in the numerator, leaving error <span class=\"m\">O(h^2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "loss-gradients": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"where-every-backward-pass-begins\" data-h=\"3\">Where every backward pass begins</h3>\n<p>Backpropagation starts at the loss. Before any gradient can flow back through a network, we need the derivative of the loss with respect to the model's final output. This lesson derives that starting gradient for the three losses that cover most of machine learning: mean squared error for predicting numbers, binary cross-entropy for yes-or-no questions, and softmax cross-entropy for choosing one class out of many. The surprise at the end is that all three give the same simple answer, <strong>prediction minus target</strong>, and this is not an accident.</p>\n<p>Imagine a weather app that says \"70% chance of rain\". If it rains, the forecast was decent but could have been more confident; if it stays dry, it was quite wrong. A loss function turns this judgement into a number, and its gradient tells the forecaster which way, and how hard, to adjust. The ideal gradient is large when the forecast is badly wrong and small when it is nearly right.</p>\n<h3 id=\"mean-squared-error\" data-h=\"3\">Mean squared error</h3>\n<p>For a real-valued prediction <span class=\"m\">\\hat y</span> and target <span class=\"m\">y</span>, the squared error loss is <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(\\hat y - y)^2</span>. The factor <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> is there only to cancel the 2 from differentiation:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\hat y} = \\hat y - y</span>\nFor linear regression <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = \\mathbf w^T\\mathbf x + b</span>, the chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}L = (\\hat y - y)\\mathbf x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial b = \\hat y - y</span>. Say <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (1, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w = (0.5, 0.5)</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 1.5</span>, the error is <span class=\"m\">-0.5</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}L = (-0.5, -1)</span>. The negative gradient points toward larger weights, which would raise the prediction toward 2. The bigger input gets the bigger correction, because it had more influence on the mistake.</p>\n<p>For a whole dataset with design matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span> (one example per row), <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac1{2n}\\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}L = \\frac1nX^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y)</span>: the average of the per-example gradients.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mean squared error (MSE)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The average of squared differences between predictions and targets, <span class=\"m\">\\frac1n\\sum_i(\\hat y_i - y_i)^2</span>, often with an extra factor <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>. Its gradient with respect to each prediction is proportional to the error <span class=\"m\">\\hat y_i - y_i</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-sigmoid-and-its-tidy-derivative\" data-h=\"3\">The sigmoid and its tidy derivative</h3>\n<p>For yes-or-no problems we want a probability between 0 and 1. A linear score <span class=\"m\">z = \\mathbf w^T\\mathbf x + b</span>, called a <strong>logit</strong>, can be any real number, so we squash it with the sigmoid <span class=\"m\">\\sigma(z) = \\frac1{1 + e^{-z}}</span>. Its derivative has a lovely form:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sigma'(z) = \\sigma(z)\\,(1 - \\sigma(z))</span>\nAt <span class=\"m\">z = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma = 0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma' = 0.25</span>, the largest the slope ever gets. Far from zero the slope is tiny: at <span class=\"m\">z = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sigma\\approx0.9933</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'\\approx0.0066</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"binary-cross-entropy\" data-h=\"3\">Binary cross-entropy</h3>\n<p>The natural loss for a predicted probability <span class=\"m\">p = \\sigma(z)</span> and a label <span class=\"m\">y\\in\\{0, 1\\}</span> is the negative log-likelihood, or <strong>binary cross-entropy</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">L = -\\big[y\\log p + (1 - y)\\log(1 - p)\\big]</span>\nIf <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span> this is <span class=\"m\">-\\log p</span>, which is 0 when <span class=\"m\">p = 1</span> and grows without bound as <span class=\"m\">p\\to0</span>. Differentiate with respect to <span class=\"m\">p</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial p} = -\\frac yp + \\frac{1 - y}{1 - p}</span>. Now multiply by <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(z) = p(1 - p)</span>, and the fractions cancel:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z} = -y(1 - p) + (1 - y)p = p - y</span>\nWith <span class=\"m\">z = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">p\\approx0.731</span> and the gradient is <span class=\"m\">-0.269</span>, pushing <span class=\"m\">z</span> up.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-not-just-use-squared-error-on-probabilities\" data-h=\"3\">Why not just use squared error on probabilities?</h3>\n<p>Suppose the model is confidently wrong: <span class=\"m\">z = -5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">p\\approx0.0067</span>, but <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>. Cross-entropy gives <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial z = p - y\\approx-0.993</span>, a strong push. Squared error <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(p - y)^2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(p - y)\\,p(1 - p)\\approx-0.0066</span>, about 150 times weaker, because the flat sigmoid slope multiplies it. A model trained with squared error would learn very slowly from exactly the mistakes it most needs to fix. Cross-entropy's log cancels the sigmoid's flatness.</p>\n<h3 id=\"softmax-for-many-classes\" data-h=\"3\">Softmax for many classes</h3>\n<p>With <span class=\"m\">K</span> classes the model outputs a logit vector <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z\\in\\mathbb{R}^K</span> and converts it to probabilities with the <strong>softmax</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">p_i = \\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_je^{z_j}}</span>\nThe exponentials make everything positive, and dividing by the sum makes the probabilities add to 1. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = (2, 1, 0)</span>, the exponentials are about <span class=\"m\">7.389</span>, <span class=\"m\">2.718</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span>, which sum to <span class=\"m\">11.107</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p\\approx(0.665, 0.245, 0.090)</span>.</p>\n<p>The softmax Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial p_i}{\\partial z_j} = p_i(\\delta_{ij} - p_j)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\delta_{ij}</span> is 1 if <span class=\"m\">i = j</span> and 0 otherwise. With a one-hot target <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y</span> (1 for the correct class <span class=\"m\">c</span>, 0 elsewhere), the cross-entropy is <span class=\"m\">L = -\\sum_iy_i\\log p_i = -\\log p_c</span>, and combining it with the Jacobian gives\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}L = \\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span>\nIn the example with correct class 1 (the first), the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(0.665 - 1, 0.245, 0.090) = (-0.335, 0.245, 0.090)</span>: raise the correct logit, lower the others in proportion to how much probability they stole. The entries always sum to zero.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Softmax</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The function <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)_i = e^{z_i}/\\sum_je^{z_j}</span>, turning a vector of real scores (logits) into a probability distribution. Adding the same constant to every logit does not change it.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cross-entropy loss</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a predicted distribution <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> and target distribution <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = -\\sum_iy_i\\log p_i</span>. With a one-hot target it is <span class=\"m\">-\\log</span> of the probability given to the correct class.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"numerical-stability\" data-h=\"3\">Numerical stability</h3>\n<p>Computing <span class=\"m\">e^{1000}</span> overflows. Because softmax is unchanged when a constant is subtracted from all logits, implementations compute <span class=\"m\">e^{z_i - \\max_jz_j}</span> instead, and compute <span class=\"m\">\\log\\sum_je^{z_j}</span> as <span class=\"m\">m + \\log\\sum_je^{z_j - m}</span> with <span class=\"m\">m = \\max_jz_j</span>. This is why libraries offer combined \"cross-entropy from logits\" functions: they are both more stable and use the simple <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> gradient directly.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Applying softmax inside the model and then again inside a loss that expects logits. Using squared error with a sigmoid output for classification. Forgetting the <span class=\"m\">\\frac1n</span> when averaging over a batch, which silently changes the effective learning rate. Expecting the softmax-cross-entropy gradient to be <span class=\"m\">-1/p_c</span> only for the correct class: the full gradient with respect to the logits is <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> and involves every class.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The derivative of the loss with respect to the model's output starts every backward pass. MSE gives <span class=\"m\">\\hat y - y</span>, sigmoid with binary cross-entropy gives <span class=\"m\">p - y</span> with respect to the logit, and softmax with cross-entropy gives <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span>. These pairings are chosen because the log in cross-entropy cancels the flat regions of the squashing function, so confident mistakes produce strong corrections.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-compgraph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;neuron&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A logistic neuron: <span class=\"m\">z = wx + b</span>, <span class=\"m\">p = \\sigma(z)</span>, cross-entropy loss. Step backwards and watch the gradient at <span class=\"m\">z</span> become <span class=\"m\">p - y</span>.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the defaults <span class=\"m\">w = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, the logit is <span class=\"m\">z = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">p\\approx0.731</span>. Step the backward pass until <span class=\"m\">\\bar z</span> appears and confirm it equals <span class=\"m\">p - y\\approx-0.269</span>.\n2. Look at the two intermediate adjoints: <span class=\"m\">\\bar p = -1/p\\approx-1.368</span>, and the sigmoid node multiplies it by <span class=\"m\">p(1 - p)\\approx0.197</span>.\n3. Drag <span class=\"m\">b</span> to <span class=\"m\">-4</span> so the model is confidently wrong (<span class=\"m\">z = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">p\\approx0.12</span>). The gradient at <span class=\"m\">z</span> grows toward <span class=\"m\">-1</span>.\n4. Switch <span class=\"m\">y</span> to 0 and confirm <span class=\"m\">\\bar z</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">+p</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> however wildly <span class=\"m\">\\bar p</span> changes, the product with <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span> always lands on <span class=\"m\">p - y</span>, which is the cancellation derived in the article. The weight gradient is then just <span class=\"m\">(p - y)\\,x</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">L = \\tfrac12(\\hat y - y)^2\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\hat y} = \\hat y - y\\qquad\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}\\tfrac1{2n}\\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2 = \\tfrac1nX^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sigma(z) = \\frac1{1 + e^{-z}},\\qquad\\sigma'(z) = \\sigma(z)(1 - \\sigma(z))</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L = -[y\\log p + (1 - y)\\log(1 - p)],\\ p = \\sigma(z)\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z} = p - y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p_i = \\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_je^{z_j}},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial p_i}{\\partial z_j} = p_i(\\delta_{ij} - p_j),\\qquad\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\Big(-\\sum_iy_i\\log p_i\\Big) = \\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derivative of the sigmoid</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(z) = \\sigma(z)(1 - \\sigma(z))</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma(z) = (1 + e^{-z})^{-1}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(z) = e^{-z}(1 + e^{-z})^{-2} = \\frac1{1 + e^{-z}}\\cdot\\frac{e^{-z}}{1 + e^{-z}}</span>. The second factor is <span class=\"m\">1 - \\sigma(z)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Softmax cross-entropy gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sum_iy_i = 1</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\left(-\\sum_iy_i\\log p_i\\right) = \\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\log p_i = z_i - \\log\\sum_ke^{z_k}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L = -\\sum_iy_iz_i + \\left(\\sum_iy_i\\right)\\log\\sum_ke^{z_k} = -\\sum_iy_iz_i + \\log\\sum_ke^{z_k}</span>. Differentiating with respect to <span class=\"m\">z_j</span> gives <span class=\"m\">-y_j + e^{z_j}/\\sum_ke^{z_k} = p_j - y_j</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Logistic regression gradient for <span class=\"m\">w = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Logit <span class=\"m\">z = wx + b = 1</span>, probability <span class=\"m\">p = \\sigma(1)\\approx0.731</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial z = p - y\\approx-0.269</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial w = (p - y)x\\approx-0.538</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial b\\approx-0.269</span>. Gradient descent increases both, raising <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Batch MSE gradient with <span class=\"m\">X = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\1 &amp; 1\\\\1 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w = (0, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y = (1, 2, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Predictions <span class=\"m\">X\\mathbf w = (0, 1, 2)</span>; residuals <span class=\"m\">X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y = (-1, -1, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">X^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y) = (-1 - 1 + 0,\\ 0 - 1 + 0) = (-2, -1)</span>.</li>\n<li>Divide by <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}L = (-\\tfrac23, -\\tfrac13)</span>. Both the intercept and the slope should increase.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A three-class classifier outputs logits <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z = (0, 0, \\ln2)</span> and the correct class is the third. (a) Compute <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> and the loss. (b) Compute <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}L</span>. (c) Show that adding 5 to every logit changes neither <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> nor the gradient.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) The exponentials are <span class=\"m\">1, 1, 2</span>, summing to 4, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p = (0.25, 0.25, 0.5)</span> and <span class=\"m\">L = -\\ln0.5 = \\ln2\\approx0.693</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y = (0.25, 0.25, -0.5)</span>. The entries sum to zero.\n(c) Every exponential is multiplied by <span class=\"m\">e^5</span>, which cancels between numerator and denominator, so <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> and hence <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> are unchanged.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>These formulas are the output-layer \"error signals\" in every classifier and regressor. PyTorch's <code>CrossEntropyLoss</code> and <code>BCEWithLogitsLoss</code> take raw logits for stability and internally use the <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> gradient. Label smoothing simply replaces the one-hot <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y</span> with a softened distribution, and the gradient stays <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The log-sum-exp trick, <span class=\"m\">\\log\\sum_je^{z_j} = m + \\log\\sum_je^{z_j - m}</span>, prevents floating-point overflow. In 64-bit floats <span class=\"m\">e^{710}</span> already overflows to infinity, while logits of that size are perfectly possible during unstable training.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weather services score probability forecasts with log loss. A forecast of 90% rain on a dry day costs <span class=\"m\">-\\ln0.1\\approx2.30</span>, while 60% costs <span class=\"m\">-\\ln0.4\\approx0.92</span>. The gradient <span class=\"m\">p - y</span> tells the forecasting model exactly how far to move its probability after each day.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.25|1/4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sigma(0) = 0.5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(0) = 0.5\\times0.5</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A logistic model predicts <span class=\"m\">p = 0.8</span> but the label is <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial z</span> for binary cross-entropy?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.8|4/5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">p - y = 0.8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first softmax probability for logits <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.5|1/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Equal logits give equal probabilities.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The entries of the softmax cross-entropy gradient <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> always sum to...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p</span> and a one-hot <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf y</span> sum to 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is cross-entropy preferred over squared error for sigmoid outputs?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Its gradient <span class=\"m\">p - y</span> stays large when the model is confidently wrong.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It is always smaller than squared error.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">It does not need a gradient.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Squared error is multiplied by the tiny <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span> in the flat regions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(\\hat y - y)^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial\\hat y</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\hat y - y = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "backprop": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-credit-assignment-problem\" data-h=\"3\">The credit assignment problem</h3>\n<p>A neural network can have millions of weights, and after it makes a prediction we get a single number back: the loss. Training means nudging every weight in the direction that lowers that number. So for every weight we need to know how much the loss would change if that weight changed a little. This is called the <strong>credit assignment problem</strong>: which weights deserve the blame for the mistake, and by how much?</p>\n<p>Imagine a factory where raw material passes through several workstations before a final inspector scores the product. If the score is bad, the inspector does not interview every worker separately. Instead, the last workstation is told how the product should have been different; it works out how its own settings contributed and how its input should have been different, and passes that message back to the workstation before it. Each station only needs the message from the next station and what it remembers about its own work. Backpropagation is exactly this message passing, and you already have every ingredient: the vector chain rule, the VJPs of linear and elementwise layers, and the loss gradients of the previous lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-network\" data-h=\"3\">The network</h3>\n<p>Consider a two-layer network with input <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf z_1 = W_1\\mathbf x + \\mathbf b_1,\\qquad\\mathbf h = \\sigma(\\mathbf z_1),\\qquad\\mathbf z_2 = W_2\\mathbf h + \\mathbf b_2,\\qquad L = \\ell(\\mathbf z_2, \\mathbf y)</span>\nHere <span class=\"m\">\\sigma</span> is an elementwise activation such as ReLU, and <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span> is a loss such as squared error or softmax cross-entropy applied to the output logits <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_2</span>. The <strong>forward pass</strong> computes these quantities in order and, crucially, stores <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span>, because the backward pass will need them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-backward-pass-one-layer-at-a-time\" data-h=\"3\">The backward pass, one layer at a time</h3>\n<p>Write <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell = \\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf z_\\ell</span> for the <strong>error signal</strong> at layer <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span>, the gradient of the loss with respect to that layer's pre-activations.</p>\n<p><strong>Output layer.</strong> From the previous lesson, <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_2 = \\hat{\\mathbf y} - \\mathbf y</span> for squared error, or <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span> for softmax cross-entropy.</p>\n<p><strong>Second weights.</strong> The layer <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_2 = W_2\\mathbf h + \\mathbf b_2</span> is linear, so its weight gradient is an outer product and its bias gradient is the error signal itself: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_2} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_2\\mathbf h^T</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_2} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_2</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Back into the hidden layer.</strong> The same linear layer sends the error back through the transposed weights: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf h} = W_2^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_2</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Through the activation.</strong> The activation is elementwise, so <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_1 = \\bar{\\mathbf h}\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_1)</span>. Note that <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span> is evaluated at the stored pre-activation <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_1</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>First weights.</strong> Again an outer product: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_1} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_1\\mathbf x^T</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_1} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_1</span>.</p>\n<p>For a deeper network the middle three steps simply repeat: transpose-multiply, then multiply elementwise by the activation slope, then form the outer product with the layer's input. That is the whole algorithm.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Error signal (delta)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell = \\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf z_\\ell</span>, the gradient of the loss with respect to the pre-activations of layer <span class=\"m\">\\ell</span>. Backpropagation computes it from <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1}</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell = (W_{\\ell+1}^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1})\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_\\ell)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Backpropagation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The algorithm that computes the gradient of a network's loss with respect to all weights by one forward pass (storing activations) followed by one backward pass that propagates error signals from the output layer to the input layer. It is reverse-mode automatic differentiation specialised to layered networks.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-complete-example-with-numbers\" data-h=\"3\">A complete example with numbers</h3>\n<p>Use the smallest possible network, with one unit per layer: <span class=\"m\">a = w_1x + b_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = \\operatorname{ReLU}(a)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = w_2h + b_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(\\hat y - y)^2</span>. Take <span class=\"m\">x = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_1 = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_2 = 1.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = 0.5</span> and target <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>.</p>\n<p>Forward: <span class=\"m\">a = 3 - 1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 3 + 0.5 = 3.5</span>, and <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(2.5)^2 = 3.125</span>.</p>\n<p>Backward: the output error is <span class=\"m\">\\delta_2 = \\hat y - y = 2.5</span>. The second-layer gradients are <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_2 = \\delta_2h = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_2 = 2.5</span>. The message to the hidden unit is <span class=\"m\">\\bar h = w_2\\delta_2 = 3.75</span>. ReLU is active because <span class=\"m\">a = 2 &gt; 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\delta_1 = 3.75</span>. The first-layer gradients are <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_1 = \\delta_1x = 5.625</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_1 = 3.75</span>.</p>\n<p>Now take one gradient descent step with learning rate <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>: <span class=\"m\">w_1 = 1.4375</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_1 = -1.375</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_2 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = 0.25</span>. The new forward pass gives <span class=\"m\">a = 2.15625 - 1.375 = 0.78125</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = 0.78125</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 1.03125</span>, so the loss falls from <span class=\"m\">3.125</span> to about <span class=\"m\">0.0005</span>. One step of backprop and descent almost solved this tiny problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"mini-batches\" data-h=\"3\">Mini-batches</h3>\n<p>In practice we process a batch of <span class=\"m\">n</span> examples at once by stacking them as the columns of a matrix <span class=\"m\">X</span>. Every formula keeps its shape, and the outer products become matrix products that automatically <strong>sum over the batch</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_1} = \\Delta_1X^T</span>, where column <span class=\"m\">i</span> of <span class=\"m\">\\Delta_1</span> is the error signal of example <span class=\"m\">i</span>. The bias gradient is the sum of the columns of <span class=\"m\">\\Delta_1</span>. If the loss is an average, the <span class=\"m\">\\frac1n</span> is already inside <span class=\"m\">\\Delta</span>. These matrix products are exactly what GPUs are built to do quickly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"vanishing-and-exploding-gradients\" data-h=\"3\">Vanishing and exploding gradients</h3>\n<p>Look at the recursion <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell = (W_{\\ell+1}^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1})\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_\\ell)</span>. The error signal at an early layer is a product of many factors, one per layer. If each factor shrinks the signal, it decays exponentially with depth. The sigmoid's slope is at most <span class=\"m\">0.25</span>, so through 10 sigmoid layers the signal can shrink by <span class=\"m\">0.25^{10}\\approx10^{-6}</span>, and the early layers barely learn. That is the <strong>vanishing gradient</strong> problem. If the weight matrices have large singular values instead, the signal grows exponentially and training blows up: <strong>exploding gradients</strong>.</p>\n<p>Modern networks fight this in several ways: ReLU activations whose slope is exactly 1 when active, careful weight initialisation (Xavier or He) that keeps the scale of signals roughly constant, normalisation layers, residual connections <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h_{\\ell+1} = \\mathbf h_\\ell + f(\\mathbf h_\\ell)</span> whose Jacobian <span class=\"m\">I + J_f</span> always contains an identity path, and gradient clipping for explosions.</p>\n<div class=\"box warn\"><div class=\"box-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"box-title\">Common mistakes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Evaluating <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span> at the activation <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span> instead of the pre-activation <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z</span> (for sigmoid you may write <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(\\mathbf z) = \\mathbf h\\odot(1 - \\mathbf h)</span>, which is fine, but do not plug <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span> into <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'</span>). Writing <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x\\boldsymbol\\delta^T</span> instead of <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta\\mathbf x^T</span>; always check the result has the shape of <span class=\"m\">W</span>. Forgetting to sum bias gradients over the batch. In frameworks, forgetting to reset accumulated gradients between steps, so they keep adding up.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Backprop runs the network forward, stores the activations, and sends an error signal backwards. At each layer it forms the weight gradient as the outer product of the error signal with the layer's input, passes the signal through the transposed weights, and multiplies elementwise by the activation slope. The cost is about that of a forward pass. Because the signal is a product over layers, it can vanish or explode, which shapes the design of deep networks.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g05-compgraph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;mlp&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A one-unit-per-layer network <span class=\"m\">h = \\operatorname{ReLU}(w_1x + b_1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = w_2h + b_2</span> with squared-error loss. Step the backward pass through both layers.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. At the defaults, confirm the forward values from the article: <span class=\"m\">a = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 3.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = 3.125</span>.\n2. Press Backward step one node at a time and check <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_2 = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar h = 3.75</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_1 = 5.625</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_1 = 3.75</span>.\n3. Drag <span class=\"m\">b_1</span> to <span class=\"m\">-3.5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">a = -0.5</span> and the ReLU is off. Press All gradients: <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_1</span> become 0.\n4. Set <span class=\"m\">w_2</span> near 0 and watch the gradients reaching the first layer shrink, because <span class=\"m\">\\bar h = w_2\\delta_2</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> gradients reaching the first layer are products of everything downstream: the output error, the second weight and the ReLU slope. When any factor is 0 (a dead ReLU) or small, the first layer stops learning. This is the vanishing-gradient mechanism from the article in its smallest form.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_L = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf z_L}\\quad(=\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y\\ \\text{or}\\ \\hat{\\mathbf y} - \\mathbf y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_\\ell} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell\\,\\mathbf h_{\\ell-1}^T,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_\\ell} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell\\qquad(\\mathbf h_0 = \\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell} = \\left(W_{\\ell+1}^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1}\\right)\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_{\\ell})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{batch: }\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_\\ell} = \\Delta_\\ell H_{\\ell-1}^T,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_\\ell} = \\Delta_\\ell\\mathbf 1</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Backpropagation computes the exact gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a layered network with differentiable activations and loss, the recursion above produces exactly <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_\\ell</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf b_\\ell</span> for every layer, using one forward and one backward pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-title\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By induction from the output. <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_L</span> is correct by definition. If <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1}</span> is correct, then since <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_{\\ell+1} = W_{\\ell+1}\\sigma(\\mathbf z_\\ell) + \\mathbf b_{\\ell+1}</span>, the vector chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial\\mathbf z_\\ell = J^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1}</span> with <span class=\"m\">J = W_{\\ell+1}\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_\\ell))</span>, which equals <span class=\"m\">(W_{\\ell+1}^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1})\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_\\ell)</span>. The weight and bias gradients then follow from the VJP of a linear layer.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Backprop through a 2-2-1 ReLU network: <span class=\"m\">W_1 = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\\\2 &amp; 1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf b_1 = \\mathbf 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">W_2 = \\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; -1\\end{bmatrix}</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (2, 1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = -2</span>, squared error</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Forward: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf z_1 = (1, 5)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h = (1, 5)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat y = 1 - 5 = -4</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(-4 + 2)^2 = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Output: <span class=\"m\">\\delta_2 = \\hat y - y = -2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_2 = \\delta_2\\mathbf h^T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 &amp; -10\\end{bmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li>Hidden: <span class=\"m\">\\bar{\\mathbf h} = W_2^T\\delta_2 = (-2, 2)</span>; both units are active, so <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_1 = (-2, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_1 = \\boldsymbol\\delta_1\\mathbf x^T = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 &amp; -2\\\\4 &amp; 2\\end{bmatrix}</span>. Check one entry: <span class=\"m\">\\hat y</span> depends on <span class=\"m\">(W_1)_{11}</span> through <span class=\"m\">h_1</span> with slope <span class=\"m\">x_1 = 2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial(W_1)_{11} = (\\hat y - y)\\cdot2 = -4</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How fast can a signal vanish through sigmoid layers?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each sigmoid layer multiplies the error signal by <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(\\mathbf z)\\le0.25</span> (ignoring the weights).</li>\n<li>Through 10 layers: <span class=\"m\">0.25^{10} = 1/1048576\\approx9.5\\times10^{-7}</span>.</li>\n<li>With ReLU, active units have slope exactly 1, so this factor disappears and the signal's size depends only on the weights.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A network computes <span class=\"m\">h = \\operatorname{ReLU}(w_1x + b_1)</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = w_2h + b_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">p = \\sigma(z)</span> with binary cross-entropy. Take <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_1 = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_2 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>. (a) Compute the forward pass and loss. (b) Compute all four parameter gradients. (c) Take one gradient step with learning rate 1 and find the new <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">h = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">p = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = -\\ln0.5\\approx0.693</span>.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">\\delta_2 = p - y = -0.5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_2 = \\delta_2h = -0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_2 = -0.5</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\bar h = w_2\\delta_2 = -1</span>; the ReLU is active, so <span class=\"m\">\\delta_1 = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\bar w_1 = \\delta_1x = -1</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\bar b_1 = -1</span>.\n(c) New parameters: <span class=\"m\">w_1 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">w_2 = 2.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_2 = -1.5</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">h = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = 7.5 - 1.5 = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">p = \\sigma(6)\\approx0.998</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every training step in PyTorch is backprop: <code>optimizer.zero_grad()</code>, a forward pass, <code>loss.backward()</code> (the backward pass above, done automatically), then <code>optimizer.step()</code>. Residual connections in ResNets and transformers exist largely to keep <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta</span> from vanishing across dozens or hundreds of layers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Backprop's memory cost is linear in depth because every activation is stored. Gradient checkpointing stores only every <span class=\"m\">k</span>-th layer's activations and recomputes the others during the backward pass; with <span class=\"m\">k\\approx\\sqrt{\\text{depth}}</span>, memory drops to about the square root of the depth for roughly one extra forward pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Large organisations assign responsibility the same way. When a product launch misses its target, the post-mortem starts with the final outcome and works backwards: how much of the shortfall came from marketing, how much from manufacturing, and within manufacturing, which supplier. Each team only needs the feedback from the team after it, just as each layer only needs <span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta</span> from the next layer.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which is the correct hidden-layer error signal?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_1 = (W_2^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_2)\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_1 = W_2\\boldsymbol\\delta_2\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf h)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_1 = \\boldsymbol\\delta_2\\mathbf x^T</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Transpose-multiply through <span class=\"m\">W_2</span>, then multiply elementwise by the activation slope at the pre-activation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">W_2</span> is <span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span>. What is the shape of <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_2 = \\boldsymbol\\delta_2\\mathbf h^T</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times5</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">5\\times3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times1</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A gradient always has the shape of the variable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_2 = (2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h = (1, 3)</span>. What is the second entry of <span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_2\\mathbf h^T = [2, 6]</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Ignoring weights, by what factor at most can two sigmoid layers shrink an error signal?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.0625|1/16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0.25^2 = 1/16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A ReLU unit has pre-activation <span class=\"m\">-0.5</span>. What gradient flows back through it?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>ReLU's slope is 0 for negative inputs, so the unit passes no gradient.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why does the forward pass store activations?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">The backward pass needs them to form weight gradients and activation slopes.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">To make the forward pass faster.</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Because the loss cannot be computed otherwise.</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\partial L/\\partial W_\\ell = \\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell\\mathbf h_{\\ell-1}^T</span> uses the stored inputs, and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_\\ell)</span> uses the stored pre-activations.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "shapes", "t": "Shapes of derivatives", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf x) = \\left(\\tfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1},\\dots,\\tfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)^T\\in\\mathbb{R}^n</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial y_i}{\\partial x_j},\\qquad J\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}\\ \\text{for}\\ g:\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}^m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\left(\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W}\\right)_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}},\\qquad \\Delta L\\approx\\sum_{i,j}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_{ij}}\\Delta W_{ij}</span></p>"}, {"l": "mc-jacobians", "t": "Jacobians, Hessians and local linear maps", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">F(\\mathbf p + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx F(\\mathbf p) + J(\\mathbf p)\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x\\ \\Rightarrow\\ J = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j},\\qquad H = J_{\\nabla f}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbf x + \\boldsymbol\\Delta)\\approx f(\\mathbf x) + \\nabla f^T\\boldsymbol\\Delta + \\tfrac12\\boldsymbol\\Delta^TH\\boldsymbol\\Delta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{area}(F(S))\\approx|\\det J|\\cdot\\text{area}(S)\\ \\text{for a small region } S</span></p>"}, {"l": "identities", "t": "The essential identities", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf a^T\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf a \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^T\\mathbf x) = 2\\mathbf x \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\|\\mathbf x\\| = \\frac{\\mathbf x}{\\|\\mathbf x\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}(\\mathbf x^TA\\mathbf x) = (A + A^T)\\mathbf x \\ \\ (= 2A\\mathbf x \\text{ if } A \\text{ symmetric})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial (A\\mathbf x)}{\\partial \\mathbf x} = A \\qquad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\|A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b\\|^2 = 2A^T(A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial W}(\\mathbf u^TW\\mathbf v) = \\mathbf u\\mathbf v^T \\qquad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(AX) = A^T \\qquad \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(X^TA) = A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\log\\textstyle\\sum_j e^{z_j} = \\operatorname{softmax}(\\mathbf z)</span></p>"}, {"l": "differentials", "t": "Matrix differentials and the trace trick", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">dL = \\operatorname{tr}(G^TdW)\\iff G = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W};\\qquad dL = \\operatorname{tr}(M\\,dW)\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W} = M^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(XY) = (dX)Y + X(dY)\\qquad d(X^T) = (dX)^T\\qquad \\operatorname{tr}(ABC) = \\operatorname{tr}(CAB)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d(X^{-1}) = -X^{-1}(dX)X^{-1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">d\\det X = \\det X\\operatorname{tr}(X^{-1}dX)\\qquad \\frac{\\partial\\log\\det X}{\\partial X} = X^{-T}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(X^TAX) = (A + A^T)X\\qquad\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial X}\\operatorname{tr}(AX^{-1}) = -X^{-T}A^TX^{-T}</span></p>"}, {"l": "vector-chain", "t": "The vector chain rule", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">J_{f\\circ g}(\\mathbf x) = J_f(g(\\mathbf x))\\,J_g(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}L = J_g^T\\,\\nabla_{\\mathbf y}L\\qquad(\\bar{\\mathbf x} = J^T\\bar{\\mathbf y})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf y = W\\mathbf x:\\quad\\bar{\\mathbf x} = W^T\\bar{\\mathbf y},\\quad\\bar W = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\mathbf x^T</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf y = \\sigma(\\mathbf x)\\ \\text{elementwise}:\\quad\\bar{\\mathbf x} = \\bar{\\mathbf y}\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf x)</span></p>"}, {"l": "autodiff", "t": "Computational graphs and automatic differentiation", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\bar v = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v},\\qquad\\bar L = 1,\\qquad\\bar v = \\sum_{c\\,\\in\\,\\text{children}(v)}\\bar c\\,\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">c = a + b:\\ \\bar a\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c,\\ \\bar b\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\qquad c = ab:\\ \\bar a\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\,b,\\ \\bar b\\mathrel{+}=\\bar c\\,a</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{forward mode: }\\dot c = \\sum_{p\\,\\in\\,\\text{parents}(c)}\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial p}\\dot p</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{L(w + h) - L(w - h)}{2h} = \\frac{dL}{dw} + O(h^2)</span></p>"}, {"l": "loss-gradients", "t": "Gradients of common losses", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">L = \\tfrac12(\\hat y - y)^2\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\hat y} = \\hat y - y\\qquad\\nabla_{\\mathbf w}\\tfrac1{2n}\\|X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y\\|^2 = \\tfrac1nX^T(X\\mathbf w - \\mathbf y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\sigma(z) = \\frac1{1 + e^{-z}},\\qquad\\sigma'(z) = \\sigma(z)(1 - \\sigma(z))</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L = -[y\\log p + (1 - y)\\log(1 - p)],\\ p = \\sigma(z)\\Rightarrow\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z} = p - y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p_i = \\frac{e^{z_i}}{\\sum_je^{z_j}},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial p_i}{\\partial z_j} = p_i(\\delta_{ij} - p_j),\\qquad\\nabla_{\\mathbf z}\\Big(-\\sum_iy_i\\log p_i\\Big) = \\mathbf p - \\mathbf y</span></p>"}, {"l": "backprop", "t": "Backprop through a neural network", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_L = \\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf z_L}\\quad(=\\mathbf p - \\mathbf y\\ \\text{or}\\ \\hat{\\mathbf y} - \\mathbf y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_\\ell} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell\\,\\mathbf h_{\\ell-1}^T,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_\\ell} = \\boldsymbol\\delta_\\ell\\qquad(\\mathbf h_0 = \\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell} = \\left(W_{\\ell+1}^T\\boldsymbol\\delta_{\\ell+1}\\right)\\odot\\sigma'(\\mathbf z_{\\ell})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{batch: }\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial W_\\ell} = \\Delta_\\ell H_{\\ell-1}^T,\\qquad\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial\\mathbf b_\\ell} = \\Delta_\\ell\\mathbf 1</span></p>"}]});
